/* Exam 3 "Hear the why": spoken explanations for the L13–L15 questions (Dr. Smith). Loaded after narration.js. */
Object.assign(NARR_Q, {
"L13-001": "Coral snake venom is a toxin and the smokestack fumes are a toxicant. A toxin is a toxic substance produced by a biological system, and snake venom comes from a snake; a toxicant is produced by, or is a by-product of, human activity, and factory emissions are man-made. Poison is only the general lay term for any agent that causes a deleterious response, and xenobiotic means any compound the organism does not normally make or expect, so neither word says where the substance came from. She said, “toxins are substances coming from biological systems. Snake venom is a toxin. A toxicant is made by man through our activities.” The tempting swap, toxicant then toxin, has the origins reversed: biological origin makes the venom the toxin, and the man-made emission is the toxicant.",
"L13-002": "A xenobiotic is a compound not normally present in or produced by the organism. That is the whole definition: it is defined by being foreign, not by being harmful, so a xenobiotic may be a toxin, a toxicant, a food additive or a medication. She said, “it just means any sort of compound that gets into an organism that's not normally produced by that organism or expected to be present in that organism.” The tempting answer, that it is always poisonous, confuses foreign with harmful; a medication is a xenobiotic and is not meant to poison anyone. Only a man-made chemical describes a toxicant, and a substance produced only by a biological system describes a toxin; a xenobiotic can be either.",
"L13-003": "The five principles are source, exposure, A D M E T, dose, and target and mechanism of action. Source is where the poison comes from. Exposure covers route, duration, single versus repeated, and frequency. A D M E T is absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion with a T for toxicity. Dose is the dose makes the poison, with the curves that give L D fifty and threshold. Target and mechanism is where the substance acts and how it causes harm. She said, “we think about the source of a toxin or toxicant. We think about how are people or critters exposed. Another principle is dose. And then the last principle is target and mechanism of action.” The therapeutic index is not a principle but a value calculated within the dose principle, and route is one factor within exposure.",
"L13-004": "The added T stands for toxicity. The kinetics of a poison follow absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion, and toxicologists add a T for toxicity because the whole point is what the compound does at its target once it is there. She said, “we're in the toxicology module here, so we're gonna add the T. So absorption, distribution, metabolism, elimination, and toxicity.” Target is the tempting choice, but target and mechanism of action is a separate principle of its own, not a letter in A D M E T. Threshold belongs to the dose-response discussion, transport is part of distribution, and time belongs to the exposure principle as duration and frequency.",
"L13-005": "A medication's toxic effect and its therapeutic effect may act at different targets. Within the target-and-mechanism principle, the place where a substance causes harm may be a different organ or molecule from its therapeutic site; acetaminophen is used for pain and fever, but its toxicity is in the liver. And it is the dose at the target, not the dose given, that determines toxicity. She said, “sometimes the target of something toxic is not the same target as its therapeutic target.” The tempting answer, that the two are distinguished only by dose, is half right: dose matters, but the target can differ as well. Toxicity is never unrelated to metabolism either, because bioactivation can create the toxic species.",
"L13-006": "Mixing chemotherapy in a hood with the wrong airflow is an occupational exposure. It is a workplace exposure, and healthcare workers can be exposed to chemotherapy, radiation, or a contaminant on a patient brought to the emergency room. The four source categories are environmental exposures, occupational exposures, critter bites and stings, and medications. She said, “do you have a risk of occupational exposures as a healthcare provider? You could be exposed to chemotherapy, mixing it up in an incorrect hood with the wrong airflow.” Environmental is the tempting category, but environmental exposure means pollution in the surroundings, such as diesel fumes from a passing bus or dust from a landfill, not a hazard of the job.",
"L13-007": "From most to least effective, the routes are intravenous, inhalation, oral, dermal. That is the descending order on the slide; intravenous delivery puts the whole dose into the blood at once. The major routes of exposure are oral, inhalation, topical and parenteral, and healthcare sees more parenteral exposure than other settings. She said, “I V is gonna be your most effective route. If it's a liquid and you can inject it, I V is the most effective route, and then you can see inhalation, oral, and dermal, they decrease in the effectiveness.” The tempting order starts with oral, because oral is the most common route of exposure; but most common is not most effective, and intravenous ranks first while dermal ranks last.",
"L13-008": "At equal doses, the oral route is the less dangerous one, because first pass removes part of the dose. An oral dose passes through the liver first; if the liver makes a less toxic product, the oral route protects. She said, “if you had equivalent doses, oral versus I V, and this compound goes through first pass effect, which is the least scary route of administration? The oral is right, it'll help protect you.” The tempting answer picks intravenous because it bypasses the stomach and gut wall; but bypassing the stomach is not protective here. An intravenous dose skips first-pass metabolism, so the whole dose reaches the body in its toxic form, and bypassing the liver is exactly what makes that route worse.",
"L13-009": "Extended-release tablets can clump and then release all at once. Her answer to immediate versus extended release was that it depends: immediate release absorbs quickly, while extended-release tablets can clump in the stomach and then dump the drug later. Vehicle and formulation both change the toxicity of an exposure; an oily vehicle behaves differently from an aqueous one, and the release form of the same drug changes the overdose. She said, “oftentimes those tablets will clump together in a person's stomach. And then all of a sudden it gets released.” The tempting answer is that extended-release tablets are always the safer overdose; they are not, because the delayed, clumped release can be as dangerous as a fast one. Neither form is always safer, and formulation does change toxicity.",
"L13-010": "Acute is less than twenty four hours, subacute is less than one month, subchronic is one to three months, and chronic is more than three months, up to years. Acute means a single day or less. Subacute means repeated exposure for up to a month. Subchronic means one to three months of repeated exposure. Chronic means three months or longer. She said, “acute is for exposure for less than twenty four hours. For animal studies, for subacute, it's less than one month. Subchronic is one to three months, and then chronic is more than three months up to years.” The pairs most easily swapped are subacute and subchronic: under a month is subacute, and the one-to-three-month window is subchronic.",
"L13-011": "The two benzene workers show that a single exposure and repeated exposure can differ in effect. Benzene is her example: one exposure gives headache and drowsiness, and chronic low-dose exposure is carcinogenic. That is the first of three points about single versus repeated exposure. She said, “it's a carcinogen, causes leukemias, so wow, getting sleepy and a headache is really different than getting leukemia.” The tempting answer is tolerance, but the daily worker still gets the headache every day, so no tolerance develops. The route did not change, since both workers inhaled the same gas; half-life was not the point of this example; and leukemia is the delayed effect of chronic exposure, not an immediate one.",
"L13-012": "The D E S daughters are an example of a delayed effect of an exposure. The exposure was in utero and the effect appeared about twenty years later, which took epidemiology to uncover. D E S, an estrogen used in the nineteen fifties to help women keep a pregnancy, caused a specific vaginal cancer in the daughters exposed in utero. She said, “we started seeing a very weird and scary phenomenon about twenty years after these women were given D E S. The female children of these women would develop a very certain kind of vaginal cancer.” The tempting answer is an immediate effect of a fast-absorbing compound; fast-absorbing compounds do give immediate effects, but D E S is her example of the opposite, a delayed effect.",
"L13-013": "The daily headache shows that a chronic exposure can also give an immediate effect each time. That is the third point on the slide: repeated exposure does not stop each exposure from producing its immediate effect, and no tolerance developed. She said, “chronic exposure may produce immediate effects after each exposure. Every time I walk into my workplace, I just get sleepy and a little bit of a headache. They're not developing any tolerance.” The tempting answer says a chronic exposure produces only delayed effects, never immediate ones; but the immediate effect keeps happening with every exposure. Tolerance did not remove it, the exposure is daily and so chronic, and half-life belongs to the frequency discussion, not this one.",
"L13-014": "Compound A has the longest half-life. The slowest fall in blood concentration means the longest time for half of the compound to be cleared. The figure gives three compounds one single dose and plots blood concentration against time: A, with a half-life of about a year, stays high; B has a half-life about equal to the dosing interval, about a day; C, with a half-life of about five hours, clears quickly. She said, “A, so its half-life is longest. It takes longer for half the compound to be cleared out of the blood. C has the shortest half-life.” The tempting mistake is to say the half-life cannot be read from a single-dose curve; the rate of decline after one dose is exactly what shows the half-life. B clears faster than A, and C clears fastest of all.",
"L13-015": "Compound C never reaches the toxic concentration, because its short half-life clears each dose before the next. With repeated quarter doses, a compound accumulates when its half-life is long compared with the dosing interval. A reaches the toxic zone by about the third dose and B by about the fourth or fifth, but C, cleared between doses, never does. She said, “how about C? It's got a really short half-life. It's clearing really quickly. Does it ever get into the toxic zone after repeated exposures? No, it doesn't.” The tempting answer is B, on the idea that a half-life equal to the dosing interval makes it level off; but B accumulates and enters the toxic zone by about the fourth or fifth dose.",
"L13-016": "Compound C could still produce a bad response through an allergic reaction, toxicity at a different target, synergy with another drug, or irritation at an injection site. An allergy needs very little drug and has nothing to do with the blood concentration. The curve shows one target, and the compound may act elsewhere. Synergy with another drug was a class suggestion she called great. Injection-site damage is an adverse response unrelated to kinetics. She said, “could you still have a bad response to it? Yeah, you could be allergic to it. Might be going to a different target site. Could synergize with other drugs.” The one that is not true is accumulation of C in the blood over the dosing: C does not accumulate; that is the whole point of its short half-life.",
"L13-017": "Metabolism that turns a compound into something more harmful than the parent is bioactivation. It is biotransformation of a potential poison to an active, harmful form; her preferred word is bioactivation. Within A D M E T the metabolism step can go two ways: bioactivation, or detoxification, which is biotransformation that eliminates the ultimate toxicant or prevents it from forming. She said, “I use the word bioactivation for that. Some people use the word toxication, when metabolism occurs to create something that's more noxious than what you were originally exposed to.” First-pass effect is the tempting answer, but first pass is where metabolism happens after an oral dose, not what the metabolism produces, and conjugation, as glucuronide or sulfate, is usually detoxifying.",
"L13-018": "The enzyme that produces NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen, is cytochrome P four fifty two E one. A normal dose is handled by two families of phase two enzymes, the glucuronosyl transferases and the sulfotransferases. When those enzymes cannot keep up, the overflow goes down the phase one P four fifty pathway, specifically cytochrome P four fifty two E one, which makes NAPQI, which is toxic to the liver. She said, “it goes down through our P four fifties, one of our phase one enzymes. Specifically P four fifty two E one, to create that nasty NAPQI, and you guys already know that the NAPQI metabolite is toxic to the liver.” The tempting answer is a glucuronosyl transferase, but glucuronidation is the phase two route that detoxifies most of the dose.",
"L13-019": "The total daily dose of acetaminophen that F D A labeling says not to exceed is four grams. Four grams in twenty four hours is the labeled maximum, and she advises her older relatives to stay below it, because the phase two enzymes that detoxify the drug may be fewer with age, so more of the dose spills into cytochrome P four fifty two E one and NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen. She said, “total daily dose of acetaminophen that we should all be sticking to, not taking more of. Four grams. That's on the F D A labeling.” Two grams is the tempting answer, because she suggests less than four grams for older adults; but two grams is lower than the labeled maximum, which is what is asked. Five hundred milligrams is one typical tablet, not a daily total.",
"L13-020": "NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen, can form below four grams a day because of a genetic variation that lowers the glucuronosyl transferases or sulfotransferases, or because alcohol has induced cytochrome P four fifty two E one. With less phase two activity, part of a normal dose is pushed through cytochrome P four fifty two E one to NAPQI. Alcohol induces the enzyme, so more of the normal dose becomes NAPQI; that was her baseball-weekend example. She said, “they may have a genetic variation of those enzymes that reduces those phase two enzymes' activity. Alcohol can induce two E one expression.” Inhibition of the enzyme by alcohol is the tempting wrong one: in her account alcohol induces, not inhibits.",
"L13-021": "The saying that the dose makes the poison is attributed to Paracelsus, who studied the toxic effects of compounds long ago. Dose is a principle of toxicology because the same compound can be harmless or lethal depending on how much reaches the target. She said, “dose makes the poison. Who said that? Supposed to be attributed to a guy named Paracelsus.” The tempting answer is Casarett and Doull, but they wrote the toxicology textbook the figures come from; they did not coin the saying. Katzung is the pharmacology textbook, and Hippocrates and Galen are not the names she gave.",
"L13-022": "The L D fifty is the dose that kills fifty percent of the animals given it. It is the lethal dose for half of the exposed population, measured in rats for the table she showed, and it is read from a population dose-response curve at fifty percent cumulative mortality; the range across compounds is enormous. She said, “this is the lethal dose that will kill off fifty percent of the animals given this compound.” The tempting confusion is with the E D fifty, the dose that produces the desired effect in fifty percent of animals, and with the T D fifty, the dose that produces a toxic but non-lethal effect in fifty percent. The lowest dose that causes any effect is the threshold dose, and the L D fifty is a dose lethal to half the population, not half of some other dose.",
"L13-023": "Botulinum toxin, with an L D fifty of one hundred thousandth of a milligram per kilogram, needs the smallest dose to kill half the animals. The table runs from ethyl alcohol at ten thousand milligrams per kilogram down through nicotine, tetrodotoxin and dioxin to botulinum toxin: the dose makes the poison, but the compound sets the scale, across nine orders of magnitude. She said, “ten thousand milligrams per kilogram for ethyl alcohol would be the L D fifty in rats. So the dose makes the poison, but you have to know what compound you're talking about too.” Ethyl alcohol is the tempting pick because its number is biggest, but the largest dose needed means the least potent poison; dioxin, second from the bottom, is a hundred times less potent than botulinum toxin.",
"L13-024": "The can is thrown away because botulinum toxin may be in it, and cooking does not destroy it. Botulinum bacteria can grow inside a sealed can of low-acid vegetables because they do not need oxygen; as they multiply they release the toxin and gas, and the gas is what makes the can squirt. The toxin, not the bacteria, is lethal, and heating does not break it down. She said, “it's not the bacteria that kills you, it's the botulinum toxin that those bacteria are making as they're growing in your can of corn. You can't heat it, cook it, and break down that botulinum toxin.” The tempting answer is that cooking would concentrate the toxin; cooking neither concentrates nor destroys it, and the point is only that it does not destroy it.",
"L13-025": "A plot of percent enzyme inhibition against dose in groups of rodents is an individual dose-response relationship. It describes the response of individuals to graded doses, here graded inhibition of two enzymes, carboxylesterase and cholinesterase, which means two targets. She said, “many chemicals have more than one effect because they have multiple targets. These organophosphate exposures are inhibiting these two different kinds of enzymes. And we could not see this if we weren't graphing it as an individual dose response curve.” The tempting answer is a population dose-response relationship, but a population curve groups responses such as deaths by dose and gives L D fifty and threshold, not enzyme inhibition per individual.",
"L13-026": "On a cumulative mortality curve, the L D fifty is read at fifty percent mortality and the threshold at the lowest dose with any effect. In the figure she read, fifty percent cumulative mortality fell at about one hundred milligrams per kilogram and the first effect at about ten milligrams per kilogram. She said, “this is where fifty percent of the animals die, and the dose here is about one hundred milligrams per kilogram. And then the threshold would be showing you where is the dose where you first start seeing an effect happening.” The tempting answer puts both readings at the peak of the mortality-frequency histogram; but the peak of the frequency plot is the most common lethal dose, not the threshold.",
"L13-027": "What separates the two curves is that only the nutrient curve has a region of homeostasis. For an essential substance such as iron, too little is harmful, deficiency and even death, the middle is the region of homeostasis, and too much is toxic. For a non-essential substance such as red wine, hormesis means a low dose may be protective and a higher dose adverse, with no region of need in between. She said, “because this is not an essential nutrient, an essential element that we need, there's really no area of homeostasis. It's illustrating the example of hormesis.” The tempting answer gives the hormetic curve the region of homeostasis; that is reversed. Both curves are U-shaped; the difference is the region of need.",
"L13-028": "Hormesis describes a substance that helps at a low dose and harms at a higher dose, without being essential. Her example is a glass or a glass and a half of red wine as possibly heart protective, then too much. Because the substance is not needed, there is no region of homeostasis; it is a U-shaped curve, and a concept toxicologists use though few others do. She said, “my glass or glass and a half of red wine is possibly protective, and then I go too far and I should not be driving. So, again, this is a U-shaped curve.” The tempting answer, essential at a low dose and lethal when absent from the diet, is the essential-nutrient U-curve with deficiency and homeostasis, not hormesis.",
"L13-029": "The linear, no-threshold dose-response is the model used for carcinogens. That is how carcinogens were long modeled: linear from zero, possibly no safe dose, on the theory that a single molecule damaging D N A could start a cancer. An S-shaped dose-response implies a threshold, a dose below which there is no effect, therapeutic or toxic, and that is probably the case for medications and most poisons. Her qualifier is possibly. She said, “that's a theory for exposure to carcinogens. Maybe it just takes one molecule of a carcinogen to cause you problems. So don't always assume everything has an area where a dose is not harmful.” Medications are the tempting answer, but medications follow the S-shaped curve with a threshold below which there is no effect.",
"L13-030": "The threshold fell because the thirty three month animals' cumulative dose was higher, so a lower daily dose crossed the threshold. With daily dosing, a longer exposure means a higher cumulative dose, so the threshold for a response falls when animals are observed longer: the bladder-tumor threshold was about seventy five parts per million at twenty four months and about forty five at thirty three months. She said, “the cumulative dose that these animals got was higher at thirty three months compared to twenty four months. That's why it didn't take as much of the dose to cause threshold.” The tempting answer is that the thirty three month animals were given a higher daily dose; the daily doses were the same groups, and only the duration differed.",
"L13-031": "The untreated thirty three month hamsters with liver tumors show that cancer is a disease of aging, so old hamsters are a poor model for liver tumors. The zero-dose group had a significant number of liver tumors on their own, and a species that gets the tumor spontaneously is the wrong model for testing whether a compound causes that tumor. She said, “is cancer the disease of the aged? Yes, cancer is a disease of the aged, usually. If we're gonna look and see if two A A F causes liver cancer, hamsters are not the best animals to use for this, because they get them anyway.” The tempting answer blames a dosing mistake in the controls, but it was a well-done study and the controls truly received no two A A F.",
"L13-032": "The margin of safety gives information about the slope, calculated as the T D one over the E D ninety nine. Comparing the dose toxic to one percent with the dose effective in ninety nine percent depends on how steep the two curves are. The therapeutic index, toxic dose over therapeutic dose, carries no slope information; both are for single doses, while the margin of exposure, the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, over the exposure, is for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds. She said, “because we use a different percentage, it gives you an idea of the slope of your dose response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not.” The therapeutic index is the tempting answer, but its numerator and denominator use the same percentage, so it says nothing about slope.",
"L13-033": "The margin of exposure is used for repeated exposures to non-therapeutic compounds. The slide marks it for repeated exposures and the therapeutic index and margin of safety for single doses. It divides the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, by the actual exposure, and it is the index for compounds that are not medicines, such as pollutants. She said, “the margin of exposure, this is something that we use truly in toxicology with exposures to something that's not therapeutic, like a pollutant, an occupational exposure, and it's also for repeated exposures.” The tempting answer is single doses of a medication compared with its toxic dose; single doses use the therapeutic index or the margin of safety instead.",
"L13-034": "The correct statement is that the target organ may not be where the concentration is highest. Target and mechanism of action: the target can be an organ or a physiological endpoint; there may be multiple targets with different toxicity and different mechanisms at each; the target organ may not be the site of highest concentration; and the dose at the target is what determines toxicity. She said, “where it's having an adverse effect may not be where the highest concentration is, which is probably a little counterintuitive.” The tempting answer says the target organ is always where the concentration is highest; the slide says the opposite. And it is the dose at the target, not the dose given, that determines toxicity.",
"L13-035": "The main discharge of pharmaceuticals into surface waters is wastewater treatment plant effluent. Current treatment does not target pharmaceuticals for removal, so the plants are the main points of discharge. Applying the source principle, unmetabolized drug and metabolites from human and veterinary use enter waste streams, and the treatment plants pass them on into surface water; unused medicine that is flushed adds to it. In class she named treatment plants as one of four sources and said the water is a source for both humans and animals. Pharmaceutical factories are the tempting answer; manufacturing is one source, but the review names treatment-plant effluent as the main discharge point. Drinking-water intakes are where the trace levels are found, not where they enter.",
"L13-036": "The two extraordinary examples are vultures harmed by diclofenac and fathead minnows harmed by ethinyl estradiol. The vulture decline in India and Pakistan and the fathead minnow collapse are the two: vultures feeding on carcasses of diclofenac-treated cattle died of renal failure because they could not excrete the drug, and fathead minnows in a lake deliberately dosed with low levels of ethinyl estradiol showed cellular and tissue responses, then a population collapse by the second year. The tempting answer is humans drinking treated water, but the review found little evidence of human harm at part-per-trillion levels. The cattle were the source of the diclofenac, not the affected organisms.",
"L13-037": "Her key is true: a compound cleared from the body before the next exposure may still result in chronic adverse effects. Compound C on the frequency figure never reaches the toxic blood concentration, yet she listed allergy, another target, synergy with other drugs and injection-site damage as ways it can still harm. That is her twist on compound C: an exposure that never accumulates can still cause an adverse response by a route that has nothing to do with kinetics. This is her practice quiz, question one, with the key she released. The tempting answer is false, on the reasoning that clearance between doses makes the compound safe; but clearance between doses rules out accumulation, not every adverse response.",
"L13-038": "Chronic exposure is repeated exposure for more than three months. Her key: chronic is more than three months, up to years. This is her practice quiz, question two, the animal-study durations: acute is less than twenty four hours, subacute is less than one month, subchronic is one to three months, and chronic is more than three months. The tempting answer is one to three months, but that window is subchronic, not chronic. Less than a month is subacute, and less than twenty four hours is acute.",
"L13-039": "The value with the formula toxic dose over therapeutic dose is the therapeutic index. Her key: the therapeutic index is the toxic dose over the therapeutic dose, with the same percentage in the numerator and the denominator. It carries no slope information, which is what separates it from the margin of safety. This is her practice quiz, question three. The tempting answer is the margin of safety, but that is the T D one over the E D ninety nine, two different percentages. The margin of exposure is the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, over the exposure, and the threshold dose is read from the curve, not calculated as a ratio.",
"L13-040": "A D M E stands for absorption, distribution, metabolism, excretion. That is her key, written out on the quiz as a short answer; toxicology adds the T for toxicity to make A D M E T. On the quiz this was a free-response item rather than multiple choice, so the exam may ask for the expansion written out. The tempting wrong expansion starts with administration, but the A is absorption, not administration. The M is metabolism, not mechanism, and dose, mechanism and exposure are other principles of toxicology, not the letters of A D M E.",
"L13-041": "Her key is false: the mechanism of a drug's toxicity is not always the same as the mechanism of its efficacy. The target of something toxic is not always the same as its therapeutic target; a medication's toxicity may act at a different target, by a different mechanism, from its therapeutic effect. This is her practice quiz, question five, the target-and-mechanism principle. The tempting answer is true, on the idea that one drug has one mechanism; but the toxic target can differ from the therapeutic target, which is exactly why the statement fails.",
"L13-042": "Her key is false: the concentration of a medication in the body is not always highest at its target site. The target organ may not be the site of highest concentration, and the dose at the target is what determines toxicity. Where a compound has its adverse effect may not be where its concentration is highest; the kidney can be more sensitive than muscle at a lower concentration. This is her practice quiz, question six, from the target-and-mechanism slide. The tempting answer is true, because it feels intuitive that harm happens where the drug is most concentrated; her slide says the opposite, and that counterintuitive point is the one being tested.",
"L13-043": "The greatest source of pharmaceuticals in those waters is consumption of medications by humans and the resulting waste streams. Her key: the drug and its metabolites that people excrete after normal use reach the waste stream and the treatment plants. This is her practice quiz, question seven, the source principle applied to the article; the greatest source is ordinary human use, excreted into the waste stream. Flushing unused medications down the toilet is the tempting answer, because she discussed it, but it is a smaller contributor than everyday excretion. Medications leaching from landfills and pharmaceutical manufacturing are sources too, but not the greatest.",
"L13-044": "Her key is false: the dose of pharmaceuticals people get from drinking water does not exceed the therapeutic daily dose. Drinking-water levels are far below a therapeutic daily dose. This is her practice quiz, question eight, the dose principle applied to the article: human exposure through drinking water is far below a therapeutic dose, and the wildlife, living in the water, is the population at risk. The tempting answer is true, on the alarm that drugs are in the water at all; but the levels found are far below a therapeutic dose, so a person drinking the water never approaches what a prescription delivers.",
"L13-045": "The authors' conclusion is that more data are needed to determine whether pharmaceuticals in water are adversely affecting human health. Her key: the authors call for more data on human health effects. This is her practice quiz, question nine. Wildlife effects are documented; whether trace levels harm human health is not settled. The tempting answer is that pharmaceuticals in waters are adversely affecting human health; the authors did not conclude that, because human exposure is far below therapeutic doses. The claim that pharmaceuticals are not affecting aquatic wildlife fails too, because the article's wildlife examples show adverse effects, and since one answer is correct, no answers are correct is wrong.",
"L13-046": "The five principles of toxicology are source, exposure, A D M E T, dose, and target and mechanism of action. That is her key, written out on the quiz as a short answer; on the quiz this was a free-response list, so the exam may ask for it written out. This is her practice quiz, question ten. The tempting list swaps in route, metabolism and receptor; but route is part of exposure, metabolism is part of A D M E T, and receptor is not the principle's name. Toxin, toxicant, xenobiotic and poison are the definitions, not the principles, and half-life, L D fifty and threshold are values inside the exposure and dose principles.",
"L14-001": "Compound A is the more potent. The compound that produces the measured response at the lower dose is the more potent one, and on the left panel of the slide the response to A starts at a lower dose than it does for B. This is the comparison she expected the class to make already from Doctor Gottlieb's lectures. She said, “I think you guys are able to tell me already which compound is most potent. A, because you start getting that effect, the response that we're measuring, at a lower dose compared to B.” B is the tempting pick only if you read the curves backwards; B needs a higher dose before the response appears, so it is less potent.",
"L14-002": "C is more potent at low doses, and D is more potent once the curves cross. Her answer is that you cannot say C is always more potent; at low doses it is, but C's curve bends over while D's continues linearly across the doses tested, so after the lines cross D is the more potent compound. She said, “you can't say that C is always more potent than D. You can in the lower doses. But once you get to a certain point where it's leveled off and the line is crossing D's dose response line, you would say that D is more potent than C.” The tempting answer is C at every dose, because C responds first; but C's response levels out with increasing dose, so above the crossing point D gives more response.",
"L14-003": "C's curve could bend over because its receptors are fully saturated, because the enzymes that bioactivate it are saturated, because the gut transporter that absorbs it, such as P-glycoprotein, is saturated, or because it changes the p H of the stomach as its dose rises. Saturated receptors was the first class answer she accepted: all the binding sites are occupied and more dose gives no more response. She said, “we've talked about target, tissue, metabolism, distribution, absorption issues. So all these things could be causing compound C's potency to change with an increasing dose.” The one that does not belong is D having a longer half-life than C: half-life was not among the explanations, and the question is why C's own response stops rising.",
"L14-004": "A study that stops too early risks missing that the potencies change places at higher doses. Her point: if the doses are cut off before C's curve bends over, nobody sees where C's curve is going and the reversal in potency is missed. She said, “if we did not extend the doses far enough, let's say we cut off dosing about right here, and we weren't quite sure where the curve of C was going, we might miss the fact that their potency basically changes places.” The tempting answer is the threshold dose of D, but the threshold is at the low end of the curve, which the study did cover. The L D fifty was not the concern, the shape of the response curve was; and the high doses are exactly where C and D cross, so they are not the ones that do not matter.",
"L14-005": "From the lowest doses to the highest, the three lines are the effective dose, the toxic dose, and the lethal dose. Effective or therapeutic dose comes first, then a toxic response, then a lethal dose. The slide labels the therapeutic response E D, then T D, and finally L D: three lines of increasing response with increasing dose for one compound. She said, “effective or therapeutic dose is first, then a toxic response, and then a lethal dose. So again, this is increasing dose with increasing response, and we've got three lines, effective, toxic, or lethal.” The tempting order puts the toxic dose before the effective dose, but the therapeutic response appears before the toxic one; and toxicity precedes lethality as dose rises.",
"L14-006": "Non-parallel lines suggest that different targets and mechanisms produce the compound's three outcomes. Her reading of the slide: when the effective, toxic and lethal lines are not parallel, the mechanism producing effectiveness differs from the one producing toxicity and from the one producing death. She said, “if your dose response curves are not linear, then it would lead you to believe that the mechanisms leading to those three particular outcomes are different, so it's causing toxicity in a different way than it's causing effectiveness.” The tempting answer is that one mechanism at one target produces all three outcomes; but one mechanism at one target would be expected to give parallel lines.",
"L14-007": "The therapeutic index is conventionally the T D fifty over the E D fifty. The toxic dose in fifty percent of the animals over the therapeutic dose in fifty percent of the animals is the usual convention. It does not have to be fifty percent, but the numerator and the denominator must use the same percentage. She said, “most of the time we're using the toxic dose in fifty percent of the animals over the therapeutic dose of fifty percent of the animals. That's usually the convention of how we do therapeutic index.” The tempting answer flips it to E D fifty over T D fifty; the toxic dose goes in the numerator. T D one over E D ninety nine is the margin of safety, and the threshold is not part of the therapeutic index at all.",
"L14-008": "When a percentage other than fifty is used, the numerator and the denominator must use the same percentage. Her rule: the percentage can differ from fifty, but it must be the same for the toxic dose and the therapeutic dose. She said, “it doesn't always have to be fifty percent. It could be a different percentage, but the numerator and the denominator need to have the same percentage.” The tempting answer is a toxic dose at one percent and an effective dose at ninety nine percent, but that pairing of different percentages defines the margin of safety, not the therapeutic index. The two percentages are equal, not higher for the toxic dose, and the margin of exposure uses the N O A E L and an exposure, not percentages of animals.",
"L14-009": "The margin of safety gives an idea of the slope, because it uses two different percentages, the T D one and the E D ninety nine. Her words: the margin of safety is the toxic dose for one percent of the animals over the effective dose of ninety nine percent, and that difference in percentage is what shows the slope. She said, “because we use a different percentage, it gives you an idea of the slope of your dose response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not.” The therapeutic index is the tempting answer, but it looks at one point on each graph, T D fifty and E D fifty, and gives no slope information. The margin of exposure compares a no-effect dose with a measured exposure, and the margin of safety was built precisely to carry slope information without a graph.",
"L14-010": "The margin of exposure is the value used for repeated exposures to a non-therapeutic compound. Her description: used truly in toxicology, for something that is not therapeutic, such as a pollutant or an occupational exposure, and for repeated exposures. It is defined as the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, over the chronic exposure. She said, “the margin of exposure, this is something that we use truly in toxicology with exposures to something that's not therapeutic, like a pollutant, an occupational exposure, and it's also for repeated exposures.” A single dose of a medication is the tempting answer, but single doses of medications use the therapeutic index or the margin of safety.",
"L14-011": "The N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, comes from animal studies: it is a dose at which the animals had no adverse response. It is generated in animal exposure studies of the compound, and it goes over the exposure that humans, or a wild animal of concern, are thought to be getting; she pronounces it Noel. She said, “it stands for the no observable adverse effect level. That is a dose that we used in animal exposure studies for this compound, that the animals that we exposed did not have an adverse response.” The tempting answer is human surveys of the dose people tolerate; but the N O A E L is an animal-study value, and the human side is the exposure in the denominator.",
"L14-012": "The denominator is the total exposure the people at the site are estimated to have received. The denominator of the margin of exposure is the exposure, calculated as how much total exposure a human, or a critter in the wild, has gotten to the compound; the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, from animal studies is the numerator. She said, “the Noel is what we generate in animal exposure studies, and the denominator of exposure is we calculate, we try to figure out how much total exposure has a human gotten to that compound.” The tempting answer puts the N O A E L in the denominator, but it is the numerator. And a pollutant has no therapeutic dose, which is exactly why the margin of exposure exists.",
"L14-013": "The margins of exposure help by letting the assessor compare the values and clean up the more dangerous compound. The real value of these numbers is comparing one compound's value with another's to estimate how bad each is. She said, “you'd calculate that margin of exposure and figure out which compound is more dangerous to humans being exposed to it. And therefore you might decide to spend your money to clean up that more dangerous compound if you had to make a choice.” The tempting answer is to clean up the compound with the highest N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level; but a high N O A E L by itself only means the animals tolerated a high dose, and the ratio with exposure is what matters.",
"L14-014": "A drug with a narrow therapeutic index has a toxic dose close to its therapeutic dose. Her definition of narrow: the toxic dose is not too much higher than the therapeutic dose, so there is more chance of toxicity. A medication with a wide therapeutic index has less chance of toxicity, and the index tells how much error can be tolerated in dosing. She said, “less chance of toxicity for the drug with a wide therapeutic index. Drugs with a narrow therapeutic index means that the toxic dose is not too much higher than that therapeutic dose.” The tempting answer is that the narrow-index drug has a steeper effective-dose curve; but the therapeutic index carries no slope information at all.",
"L14-015": "The therapeutic index is the T D fifty over the E D fifty, the margin of safety is the T D one over the E D ninety nine, and the margin of exposure is the N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, over the chronic exposure. The therapeutic index is toxic dose over therapeutic dose at fifty percent: one point on each graph, no slope information. The margin of safety uses different percentages top and bottom, so it reflects the slope. She said, “therapeutic index is looking at efficacy compared to toxicity, usually using fifty percent of animals, just looking at one point in the graph.” The pair most easily confused is the index and the margin of safety: same percentage on top and bottom is the index, two different percentages is the margin of safety.",
"L14-016": "The kidney is more sensitive, and harm need not occur where concentration is highest. Her example: higher concentration in muscle with no adverse effect there, damage in the kidney at a lower concentration, which is counterintuitive. She said, “where it's having an adverse effect may not be where the highest concentration is, which is probably a little counterintuitive. Maybe the kidney's just super sensitive compared to the muscle.” The tempting answer is that damage always occurs where the concentration is highest, so the kidney level was misread; that is the intuition she said is wrong. Nothing is wrong with the measurement; the organs differ in sensitivity, and there is still a dose-response curve at the kidney.",
"L14-017": "The compound damages the kidney only when its concentration in the kidney gets high enough. Even a super-sensitive site has a dose-response curve; the dose at the target determines the toxicity. A sensitive organ is still unharmed if the compound does not reach a high enough concentration there. She said, “let's say the kidney is super sensitive, but if that compound doesn't get high enough in the kidney, it may not cause a problem. You still have a dose response curve even right there at the kidney.” The tempting answer is that a sensitive organ is damaged at any dose, as if sensitivity removed the need for a threshold; sensitivity lowers the dose needed, but the concentration at the kidney still has to be high enough.",
"L14-018": "The kidney and liver mechanisms are likely different. With multiple targets, the compound is likely causing damage to the two organs in different ways: one mechanism in the kidney, one in the liver. She said, “let's say the compound affects the kidney and the liver. It's likely to be causing damage to those two different organs in different ways. One mechanism in the kidney, one mechanism in the liver.” The tempting answer is that the same mechanism must operate in both organs, but different targets usually mean different mechanisms. Both are targets, since concentration does not decide which organ is harmed; we may not have identified all the places a compound hits; and toxic targets can differ from the therapeutic target.",
"L14-019": "Over-diuresis is therapy gone too far, while ototoxicity comes from an unrelated target. Her two examples: diuretics taking too much fluid off are therapy gone too far, and aminoglycoside ototoxicity is an adverse effect whose target differs from the therapeutic target and mechanism. She said, “that would be kind of a classic example of a side effect of diuretics where the therapy has gone too far. Why do drugs like our aminoglycosides cause ototoxicity? That's weird stuff, the classic example of how the target of an adverse effect could be very different than the therapeutic target and mechanism.” The tempting answer calls both the therapeutic action gone too far; but killing bacteria has nothing to do with damage to hearing.",
"L14-020": "The meeting was about drugs contaminating water, attended by government agencies such as the E P A, academics, the drug industry and other scientists. Her summary: an environmental health summit in two thousand eight, the first time these groups met about the finding that drugs are contaminating the environment, particularly water, to decide what is known, how bad the problem is, and what to do. She said, “it was an environmental health summit. Who was there? Government organizations of various kinds, E P A, academicians, pharmacy industry, other scientists that have an interest in the health of our planet.” The tempting answer is antibiotic dosing in livestock with veterinarians and farmers; livestock came up as one source, not as the subject of the summit.",
"L14-021": "A pharmacy student needs to know because patients who hear about it will ask whether the water is dangerous. Her second reason for assigning the paper: patients read or hear a podcast about drugs in the water and come to the pharmacist worried about drinking someone else's Prozac. The goal is to be able to tell that patient whether to calm down or be concerned. She said, “they will find out that we know that there's drugs in our water supply, and they might likely come to you freaking out. We're going to get you to the point where you can talk to your patient and you can help them calm down or be concerned.” The tempting answer is that pharmacists must test the tap water in their community; water testing was done by the scientists after the summit, not by pharmacists.",
"L14-022": "The discussion covers surface water, groundwater and drinking-water sources. Those are the three she defined before starting: lakes, streams and rivers; groundwater such as the Edwards Aquifer, which the class sits on top of; and the sources drinking water is drawn from. She said, “we're talking about surface water, we're talking about groundwater, and we're talking about drinking water sources.” The tempting answer narrows it to hospital wastewater and factory effluent only, but the paper looks at the waters people and wildlife are exposed to, not only the places drugs come from. Oceans were not among the three, and bottled water and rainwater were not mentioned.",
"L14-023": "The sources she named are drug and metabolites excreted by people on medications, improper disposal of unused medications down the toilet, livestock given hormones and antibiotics, and wastewater treatment plants that do not remove all drugs. Parent drug and metabolites both come out in excretion, her disposal example is unused antibiotics down the toilet, medicated livestock waste is a source, and treated water goes back to streams with drugs still in it. She said, “we take the drugs, we flush, we improperly dispose of our meds. We've got animals on feedlots. Our wastewater treatment plants don't take care of all the drugs before it gets released into the environment.” The one that does not belong is pharmaceutical manufacturing discharges: she did not raise manufacturing in class.",
"L14-024": "The water that treatment plants release is a source of exposure for both humans and wildlife. That is the answer she accepted: the treated water returns to streams, rivers and lakes, so it is a source for both. She said, “the water from flushing gets treated a bit, but right now the current mechanisms don't get all the drugs out of it before it goes back into your streams and rivers and lakes. So is that a source for humans or animals? Both, there you go.” Wildlife only is the tempting answer, but drinking-water sources draw on the same surface water and groundwater. Humans only fails because aquatic animals live in the receiving streams and lakes, and livestock are a source, not the group exposed by the plants.",
"L14-025": "Humans are exposed mostly by drinking, while wildlife are exposed by drinking, breathing, skin and eating. Her summary: for us it is usually drinking water; animals that live in or near water are exposed constantly, by drinking, by breathing through gills, by absorption across skin, and by eating other animals that live in the water. She said, “so they get exposed drinking, eating, swimming in the water. The route of exposure, drinking, eating, swimming, living in it. So that's our second principle.” The tempting answer says both are exposed mostly by drinking, but animals living in the water are exposed by several routes at once. Inhalation and injection were not named.",
"L14-026": "The concentration of most medications in drinking water was in the range of parts per trillion. That is the answer she accepted, and rarely is it higher. Estrogen can be somewhat higher along the shorelines of big cities with many young women taking oral contraceptives; she named Miami, Houston, San Diego and San Francisco. She said, “remember the concentrations of these drugs is found to be about parts per trillion. Rarely is it higher than that.” The tempting answer is parts per million, but that is far higher than what was found, and so are milligrams per liter. The levels do not approach a therapeutic concentration, and the drugs were detected, so they were not undetectable.",
"L14-027": "A D M E, dose and target are known for humans except for many metabolites, and largely unknown for wildlife. Drug development studies established A D M E, dose and mechanism in humans, but not for unknown metabolites, which have no data; and the test species are rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and pigs, not sea otters, orca whales, fish or mollusks. She said, “we don't tend to use sea otters and orca whales and fish and mollusks. We don't use those in our drug development studies, so we don't know how these drugs made for humans and tested for humans are going to act in these critters.” The tempting answer is that the data are known equally well for both; wild species are not used in drug development.",
"L14-028": "Her answer is that the patient is probably OK: at parts per trillion the intake does not approach a therapeutic dose. Her conclusion: for humans we can be OK, because even a well-hydrated person drinking contaminated water does not get up to a normal therapeutic daily dose. Sensitive populations should not be ignored, but her former work in risk assessment has not found these levels to be a problem for humans. She said, “is that gonna get you up to a normal therapeutic daily dose? No. So I think for humans we can be OK. Doesn't mean we should ignore our sensitive populations.” The tempting answer is that nothing is known about human risk, so the patient should worry; but human risk assessment has not found a problem even with sophisticated methods.",
"L14-029": "The sensitive populations she named are the fetus in utero, the elderly, children, and people with disease states or who are immunocompromised. Even though humans are probably OK, these groups should not be ignored; risk assessment on in utero exposure is very hard to do, and no articles show a child affected in utero by drugs in the water supply. She said, “maybe people that are immunocompromised, maybe with some disease states. Young in utero. Doesn't mean we should ignore our sensitive populations, which are in utero, elderly, kids, people with diseases.” The one that does not belong is athletes who drink large volumes of water: she used the well-hydrated person only to show that the dose is still far below therapeutic, not as a sensitive group.",
"L14-030": "Her evidence is frogs, some fish and alligators shifting toward female where estrogen is higher. Her example: populations changing from about half male, half female to more female in places with higher estrogen levels, a red flag. For other wildlife, such as orca whales, we do not know, because they are hard to catch and study. She said, “you'll start seeing populations of frogs and some fish and alligators changing from being like half male, half female to more female in those places where estrogen levels are higher. That's like a red flag.” The tempting answer is orca whales with measured drug levels, but she said orcas are hard to catch and study, so we do not know.",
"L14-031": "The evidence she cited is that isolated exposure studies with estrogenic compounds show the same shift. Species have been exposed to set doses of hormones or estrogen-like compounds in isolated studies and the shift to female is seen, and estrogen levels are also known to be high in those places. Other studies are still needed to test warming, and both could contribute. She said, “we've done isolated studies of some of these species, exposed them to certain doses of hormones, estrogenic type of compounds, and we do see a shift. We need to do other studies as well and see if the warming waters is causing that shift. It could be both.” The tempting answer is that temperature studies have ruled out warming water; she said more studies are needed and it could be both.",
"L14-032": "The concern she called a real problem is bacteria in waters and sediments becoming resistant to antibiotics. As antibiotics are improperly disposed of and reach the environment, the many microbes living in lake and stream sediments and beach sand survive them and become resistant, so an infection from a cut may be untreatable. She said, “a really big concern, which probably most people know about, is the fact that the bacteria in waters and the sediments are becoming resistant to the antibiotics that are out there in the environment, and that's a problem. That is a problem.” The tempting answer is fish accumulating lethal doses of analgesics, but that was not an effect she described.",
"L14-033": "Nerve damage appearing days after an organophosphate exposure is a delayed effect. Some organophosphates, depending on structure, cause neuropathies that appear days after exposure by affecting different enzymes in the nerves. She said, “some organophosphate pesticides can have caused neuropathies because they're affecting different enzymes in the nerves. So that's a delayed effect by about days, not years.” The tempting answer is an immediate effect, but the immediate effects are the typical cholinergic ones, and the neuropathy comes later. Nerve damage is systemic, not local; nothing beneficial occurs, so it is not hormetic; and tolerance is a reduced response with repeated exposure, not a late-appearing injury.",
"L14-034": "D E S is delayed by about years and the organophosphate neuropathy by about days. The D E S cancers appeared when the exposed daughters became young adults; the organophosphate neuropathy appears days after exposure. Delayed effects can take quite a lot of time. She said, “the very delayed response we saw was a very particular kind of vaginal cancer in their female offspring as those girls became young adults. So that's a delayed effect by about days, not years.” The tempting answer, days and years, is simply reversed. Both are longer than hours, the organophosphate neuropathy appears in days rather than years, and D E S took years, not minutes.",
"L14-035": "What determines whether a toxic injury is reversible is the tissue's ability to regenerate. The liver can regenerate, so many liver injuries are reversible, mild injury more than severe chronic injury; differentiated nerve cells cannot divide, so nerve injury is irreversible. Cancer and birth defects are also irreversible. She said, “the liver is really unique. It is able to heal itself, to regenerate oftentimes when it becomes damaged. Most of our nerves or C N S cells, their injury is permanent. They can't divide. They can't be regenerated.” The tempting answer is the concentration of the toxicant, but concentration determines whether injury occurs, not whether the tissue can recover.",
"L14-036": "Cancer is still irreversible because the cancerous cells were killed, not returned to normal. Treatment removes or kills the cells; the cells that became cancerous never reverted. Cancer and birth defects are considered irreversible: a cancer may be cut out, killed with x-rays or chemotherapy, and a birth defect in limb or body formation during fetal development cannot be fixed. She said, “that doesn't mean that those cells that became cancerous switched back to being normal. We killed them. We got rid of them.” The tempting answer is that cancer cannot be treated; it can be cut out, irradiated, or killed with chemotherapy, and that is precisely why the distinction matters.",
"L14-037": "A highly reactive agent like chlorine gas reacts at the site of first contact and is often never absorbed. Very reactive compounds such as chlorine gas and acids act where they first touch the body and often do not get the chance to go systemic. She said, “most toxins, they cause systemic effects unless they are highly reactive. So very reactive compounds like chlorine gas, like acids, they will cause the local effect. They often will not have a chance to go systemic.” The tempting answer makes chlorine gas a typical example of a rule that most toxins act locally; but most toxins cause systemic effects, and only the highly reactive ones stay local.",
"L14-038": "The tetraethyl lead case illustrates a poison with both a local and a systemic effect. The burn on the hand was the local effect; absorption through the burned skin and neurotoxicity was the systemic effect. She said, “they found a burn mark on her hand, where that compound had somehow gotten through the glove, burned her skin. It was absorbed through that burned skin, and then affected her central nervous system and killed her. So a neurotoxin, definitely.” The tempting answer is an indirect systemic effect of a burn through fluid loss; but here the compound itself was absorbed and acted on the nervous system, and the acid-burn fluid loss is the indirect example.",
"L14-039": "A local acid burn produces the kidney problem because fluid leaks through the damaged skin, blood volume is disturbed, and the kidneys suffer. Like a thermal burn, the skin can no longer hold fluids in; the fluid and volume disturbance is the indirect systemic effect, apart from the risk of infection. She said, “you run the risk of having your blood volume really messed up, because you're leaking fluids. So that's a systemic effect that can happen with an acid burn, just like with a thermal burn.” The tempting answer is that the acid is absorbed through the damaged skin and attacks the kidney directly; but the systemic effect is indirect, through fluid loss, not through absorbed acid.",
"L14-040": "The kinds of interaction are additive, synergistic, potentiation and antagonistic. Those are the four named on the slide and in class. People are rarely exposed to one thing at a time: contaminated or additive-laden foods, drugs, hobbies and the workplace all contribute, each exposure probably has its own mechanism and target, and when they are combined the compounds can interact in these four ways, which makes the combined outcome hard to predict. She said, “you could have additive or synergistic or potentiation or antagonistic type of interactions going on, which makes it really hard to predict how bad is it going to be for people or critters.” The one that does not belong is dispositional: dispositional describes a type of tolerance, not an interaction between compounds.",
"L14-041": "It is hard to put a number on the risk because the interactions among the compounds cannot be quantified. Her former work: additive, synergistic, potentiating and antagonistic interactions among the exposures are very hard to quantify. She said, “it's very hard to get a number that will quantify the risk for being in that place with all those multiple exposures. It's really hard to quantify these interactions, additive, synergism, potentiation, and antagonism.” The tempting answer is that concentrations cannot be measured accurately; but sampling measures the concentrations in ground, water or air, and animal studies supply a N O A E L, the no observed adverse effect level, for each compound.",
"L14-042": "Dispositional tolerance is A D M E based, and reduced responsiveness is pharmacodynamic. Her summary: dispositional tolerance lowers the amount of toxicant reaching its site; reduced responsiveness is the target itself responding less, such as receptor down-regulation. She said, “you can see how the dispositional is A D M E based and the reduced responsiveness is pharmacodynamically based, dealing with receptors.” The tempting answer separates them by drug versus pollutant, but both occur with medications and with environmental exposures. Both kinds develop with repeated exposure, reversibility is a different concept, and they are two distinct mechanisms, not one mechanism with two names.",
"L14-043": "Tolerance to carbon tetrachloride develops because it inhibits its own metabolism, so less of the toxic metabolite forms. Her surprising point: carbon tetrachloride inhibits its own metabolism rather than inducing it, partly protecting against the repeated exposure. She said, “you were probably thinking, oh, carbon tet will induce the enzymes that do its metabolism, but no, for carbon tet, it inhibits its own metabolism. It kind of protects you from that repeated exposure, sort of.” The tempting answer is induction of the enzymes so the metabolite is cleared faster; that is what she said students would expect, and carbon tetrachloride does the opposite.",
"L14-044": "Cadmium produces dispositional tolerance by inducing the metal-carrying protein that helps the kidneys eliminate it. Certain valences of cadmium induce that protein's expression, so more cadmium is cleared with repeated exposure. She said, “it's often carried in the body by a particular metal carrying enzyme, and if that metal carrying enzyme can grab that cadmium, it'll help you eliminate it via the kidneys. Cadmium, especially certain valences of cadmium, can induce the expression of that metal carrying enzyme and help you clear it as well.” The tempting answer is inhibition of its own metabolism to a toxic metabolite; that is the carbon tetrachloride example, not cadmium.",
"L14-045": "A medication that works less well at the same dose fits reduced responsiveness, by down-regulation of the target receptors. With repeated exposure the target down-regulates, for instance fewer receptors, so the tissue responds less. She said, “if you give a person a medication over time and it starts to not be working as well, you could be having reduced responsiveness because of a down regulation of the receptors that that medication is targeting.” The tempting answer is dispositional tolerance by induction of a carrier protein that clears the drug; but carrier induction is the cadmium example of dispositional tolerance, which is about less drug reaching the site, not the target responding less.",
"L14-046": "A compound lethal to one species and harmless to another shows selective toxicity. Lethal to one species but not to another is the definition. She said, “one of the terms is what we call selective toxicity. This means that an exposure could be lethal to one species, but not to another. Another term that we use in the world of toxicology is species differences. There's either a qualitative and or a quantitative difference in the way two different species respond.” Species differences is the tempting answer, but species differences means both species respond and differ in how; here one species is harmless. Individual differences are within one species, and idiosyncratic means the reason is unknown.",
"L14-047": "The arsenic skin cancer illustrates that species differences make animal results hard to extrapolate to humans. No animal model reproduces the human response, so the mechanism cannot be studied, and an animal study alone would have wrongly called the arsenic safe. She said, “we have tried and tried to find an animal that we could expose to drinking water that has arsenic in it. We can't find an animal model that mimics what happens to humans when we're exposed to arsenic in water.” The tempting answer is that rodents metabolize arsenic to a safer form before it reaches the skin; but she said the mechanism is unknown, and no explanation was given.",
"L14-048": "The explanation is found in the genetics of each individual. Individual differences within a species come down to genotype, which pharmacogenomics studies. People within one species respond to the same medication or environmental exposure in different ways, and the answer lies in each person's genetics. She said, “individual differences within a species to a drug or to an environmental exposure, the answer to why is going to be found in the genetics of each individual.” The tempting answer is species differences, but both people are the same species. Route is a principle of exposure, not the explanation for within-species variation; selective toxicity compares species; and tolerance is about repeated exposure, not two people's first responses.",
"L14-049": "Toxic endpoints may be described as on-target or off-target, organ specific or non-organ specific, idiosyncratic, and immunologically based, such as allergic reactions. On-target or off-target is the vocabulary for whether the drug is hitting the receptor it was designed for. Fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis are non-organ specific, while lungs, nervous system, liver and kidney are organ specific. Idiosyncratic, by her definition, means we do not know why it is happening. She said, “toxic endpoints may be defined as on or off target. They could be organ specific, they could be idiosyncratic. What does idiosyncratic mean? We don't know why it's happening.” The one that does not belong is hormetic: hormesis is a dose-response shape, not a category of toxic endpoint.",
"L14-050": "The left half shows the intended tissue and the right half an unintended tissue. The left is where we want the drug to act; the right is a tissue where the drug goes but is not wanted. On both sides the drug, or a metabolite of it, finds receptors: on-target means it finds the receptor it was designed for, off-target means it finds other receptors, and either can occur in either tissue. She said, “what kind of tissue is being described on the left? The intended tissue. This is where we want the drug to act in the body. And on the right here, this is an image of what's going on in an unintended tissue.” The tempting answer is the parent drug and its metabolite, but drug or metabolite activity appears on both sides.",
"L14-051": "A drug hitting its intended receptor in a tissue where that action is unwanted is called on-target, in the wrong tissue. It is classically called on-target: the drug hits the receptor it was meant to hit, in the wrong tissue. Off-target means the drug finds other receptors it was not intended to find, whether in the intended tissue or in another one. She said, “even though the drug is in the wrong tissue, when it's finding the receptor that we wanted it to find, it's still called on target. So this is a part of our toxicology vernacular that I really don't like.” Off-target is the tempting answer, and the one she herself would prefer, but off-target means an unintended receptor, in either tissue.",
"L14-052": "The most common reason for an on-target adverse effect in the intended tissue is that the dose is too high. Her rule: an on-target adverse effect in the right tissue usually means too much dose at the receptor. That can come from a deliberate overdose, an accidental dosing error, a change in pharmacokinetics such as a diseased liver that cannot clear the normal dose, or a change in receptor number. She said, “most of the time, if you're having an on target adverse effect in the right tissue, your dose is too high.” The tempting answer is that the drug crossed the blood-brain barrier; but that is on-target in the wrong tissue, the diphenhydramine example, not the intended tissue.",
"L14-053": "The dose at the receptor could have been raised by liver disease that slows clearance, by a change in receptor number at the target, by an accidental dosing error, or by a deliberate overdose. Liver disease is a change in pharmacokinetics, the change in receptor number is the slide's on-target example, and the other two are the dosing errors she listed. Her spoken example was down-regulation with the dose kept the same; on her tolerance slide down-regulation lowers the response. She said, “maybe their liver is not working as well anymore, and they can't metabolize and clear the drug. You could have a change in receptor number. So if you down regulate receptor numbers, keep the dose the same, you may be overhitting those receptors.” The one that does not belong is the drug finding a different receptor subtype: that is off-target, not a rise in dose at the intended receptor.",
"L14-054": "Diphenhydramine's drowsiness is an on-target effect in the wrong tissue. The same histamine receptor is hit, but in the brain, where it is not wanted. Newer antihistamines such as cetirizine and fexofenadine were tweaked so they do not cross the blood-brain barrier and so lack the drowsiness. She said, “it causes drowsiness because it's hitting the histamine receptors in the brain, so that's an on target adverse effect in the wrong tissue. When we developed newer antihistamines, we tweaked those molecules and we made them so that they don't cross the blood-brain barrier.” The tempting answer is an off-target effect at an unintended receptor; but the receptor is the intended one, and only the tissue is wrong.",
"L14-055": "Terfenadine's desired effect was peripheral H one receptor antagonism and its undesired effect was inhibition of cardiac potassium channels. The antihistamine effect came at peripheral H one receptors; the arrhythmias came from an unintended receptor, the cardiac potassium channels, so it is off-target, example one, and the drug is no longer on the market. She said, “we were having cardiac problems with that drug because it was inhibiting cardiac potassium channels. I was having increased levels of Seldane in my blood, so blocking even more of my potassium channels.” The tempting answer has the same two effects reversed, with the potassium channels as the desired target; H one antagonism in the brain with drowsiness is the diphenhydramine on-target example.",
"L14-056": "The R enantiomer of thalidomide treats nausea and the S enantiomer is a potent teratogen. The drug was taken as a racemic mixture, so people got both enantiomers. This is off-target, example two: an enantiomer hits an unintended receptor. Thalidomide was never officially on the market in the nineteen fifties and is used today in oncology. She said, “it's the R enantiomer that was good at helping with nausea, but the S enantiomer was a very potent teratogen. Do you guys know if we use thalidomide anymore therapeutically? Yeah, we use thalidomide now in oncology.” The tempting answer swaps the two, making S the helpful one and R the teratogen; that is reversed.",
"L14-057": "The asthma exacerbation is an off-target effect at an unintended receptor subtype: beta two receptors in airway smooth muscle. The beta-blocker example is about receptor subtypes, beta one versus beta two. This is off-target, example three. She said, “the asthma exacerbation example with the beta blockers is talking about subtypes of a receptor. So, beta one adrenergic receptors versus the beta two adrenergic receptors.” The tempting answer is the intended receptor, beta one, in the wrong tissue, the airway; but beta two is a different subtype from the intended beta one, so it is off-target, not on-target in the wrong tissue. Dose too high at beta one would be on-target.",
"L14-058": "Medicinal chemists change the part of the molecule that hits the other receptor. Her account: the lab looks at the molecule, identifies the part interacting with off-target receptors, and alters it so the next generation of the drug no longer hits them. She said, “people in the lab will go back and look at that molecule of a drug and say, oh, it's this part of the molecule that's hitting a different receptor. Let's see if we can change that part of the drug, so that maybe it won't hit the off target receptors.” The tempting answer is to raise the dose until the therapeutic effect dominates; but a higher dose would increase both effects.",
"L14-059": "Fibrosis causes loss of organ function through deposition of collagen and matrix proteins that stiffen the tissue. Fibrotic tissue is stiff: lungs lose flexibility, and in the liver the deposits interfere with bile ducts and hepatocyte function, so organ function is lost as the tissue is destroyed. She said, “some compounds can cause your body to lay down collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins in various tissues, and that's your fibrotic tissue being created. Fibrotic tissue is stiff.” The tempting answer is death of the whole organ at once from a single exposure; but function is lost gradually as stiff tissue replaces working tissue.",
"L14-060": "The agents she named as causes of pulmonary fibrosis are amiodarone, bleomycin and paraquat. Amiodarone is a cardiac antiarrhythmic medication, and it also causes liver fibrosis. Bleomycin is a chemotherapy drug. Paraquat is an herbicide, once used in suicide attempts, and death from its pulmonary fibrosis is long and slow. She said, “classic examples of compounds that cause pulmonary fibrosis are amiodarone, bleomycin. Amiodarone is a cardiac med. Bleomycin is a chemotherapy. Paraquat is actually an herbicide. Something that you are probably very familiar with that causes liver fibrosis is ethanol. We call that cirrhosis of the liver.” The one that is tempting but wrong is ethanol: ethanol is her example of liver fibrosis, cirrhosis, not lung fibrosis.",
"L14-061": "Fibrosis is non-organ specific because the same collagen and matrix proteins are laid down whatever the organ. Her answer: if you look at the cells and chemistry of fibrotic tissue, lung fibrosis and liver fibrosis look the same, so the same pathology creates it, even though particular compounds target particular organs. She said, “if you look at the cells, if you look at the chemistry of that fibrotic tissue, it's the same. So the same pathology goes on to create that fibrosis. So it's not like pulmonary fibrosis looks one way and liver fibrosis looks another.” The tempting answer is that the same compounds always cause both the lung and the liver forms; but bleomycin and paraquat affect the lung, ethanol the liver, and only amiodarone was named for both.",
"L14-062": "Mutagenicity is transmissible to offspring, while genotoxicity is any D N A damage. Genotoxicity is damage to D N A in general and can occur in somatic cells as well as germ cells; mutagenicity refers to a transmissible genetic alteration that can be passed down, and if the mutations happen in germ cells the agent is a mutagen. She said, “genotoxicity is just talking about damage to your D N A in general. And mutagenicity refers to the fact that it's transmissible. In other words, you can pass that down to your offspring. If mutations happen in your germ cells, then it's a mutagen.” The tempting answer has it reversed, with genotoxicity as the transmissible one. Genotoxicity does not always cause cancer, and the slide draws the distinction, so they are not the same thing.",
"L14-063": "The results of genotoxicity can include cancer, teratogenesis, and genetically based disorders. Cancer and teratogenesis, meaning birth defects, are named on the slide and in class, and some metabolic diseases have been traced to genetic damage. Genotoxicity is non-organ specific. She said, “the results of genotoxicity can be bad. You can have cancer, you can have teratogenesis, which is birth defects, or you can have genetically-based disorders. So some of our metabolic diseases have been traced to genetic damage.” The tempting addition is fibrosis, but fibrosis is a separate non-organ-specific endpoint, not a result of D N A damage. Dispositional tolerance does not belong either; tolerance is a reduced response with repeated exposure.",
"L14-064": "Lung and bronchial cancer ranks first in both men and women, and then prostate is second for men and breast is second for women. That is her reading of the cancer table on the slide, from the American Cancer Society, 2009, which ranks cancers by organ; most other cancers occur at about the same frequency in both sexes. She said, “the lung and bronchial cancer is highest for both men and women, and then for men it's prostate and breast it's women. And then most of the time these same cancers are happening with the same frequency in men versus women.” The tempting answer puts prostate and breast first with lung second; but lung and bronchial cancer ranks first for both, with prostate and breast second.",
"L14-065": "Cancer is a non-organ-specific endpoint because the same cellular mechanism can cause cancers in different organs. Some lung cancers share the genetic mutation seen in colon cancers; some lung and breast cancers share a mechanism; at the cell level the pathology is the same across organs. She said, “classic example would be some lung cancers are caused by the same genetic mutation as you will see in colon cancers. Some lung cancers are what we'll also see in some breast cancers, so at the cellular level, the pathology is the same.” The tempting answer is that every organ gets cancer at the same rate once adjusted for age; but the table shows the rates differ by organ, and the point is shared mechanism, not equal incidence.",
"L14-066": "The multistage model of carcinogenesis holds that cancer usually requires more than one mutation. Her one-sentence summary of the slide she skipped: it would be rare for a single mutation to go all the way to cancer. The slide lists initiation, promotion and progression; she skipped it for time and pointed to the Casarett and Doull text on AccessPharmacy. She said, “we think it takes more than one mutation to end up with somebody having cancer. It would be rare to have just one mutation going all the way to cancer. The multi-stage model of carcinogenesis helps explain that.” The tempting answer is a single mutation in one gene; but the model exists precisely to explain why one mutation is rarely enough.",
"L14-067": "The hamster study shows that the time of exposure during gestation determines the type of defect. The limb defects were expected; the other defects appeared when the mothers were dosed at other times in gestation. Teratogenesis is non-organ specific because the same exposure can produce different effects depending on when the mother is exposed. She said, “we didn't expect some of these other birth defects to be happening with this retinoid exposure in hamsters until we started giving it to the mothers at different times in their gestation.” The tempting answer is that retinoids damage only the limbs whatever the day of dosing; that was the expectation, and the study showed other defects at other times. Timing, not dose, set the type of defect.",
"L14-068": "The point is that different compounds can cause the same defect, with the fetus sensitive at different times. Her reading of the table of palate deformities: four compounds, four peak gestation days, one defect. She said, “if you give methanol at seven days of gestation to the mother, you end up with a palate deformity or cleft palate. If you give T C D D at eleven days of gestation, you get the same deformity, the cleft palate. So this is just to show you that different compounds can cause the same teratogenic effect.” The tempting answer is that methanol and T C D D share one mechanism at the same stage; she made no claim about mechanism, and the peak days differ.",
"L14-069": "For a teratogen, the mother must take in enough for the compound to pass through to the baby. Her one-line summary of the dose slide: fetal exposure comes through the mother's exposure and the compound's passage to the fetus. The fetus is exposed through the mother, so fetal exposure depends on the mother's absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion and on whether the compound crosses the placenta. She said, “dose in teratogenicity means the mother has to get enough of the compound for it to pass through into the baby.” The tempting answer is that only the time of exposure matters; timing sets the type of defect, but dose sets whether enough reaches the fetus.",
"L14-070": "The pregnancy labeling now carries how teratogenic the drug is, its effects on fertility including male fertility, and whether a lactating mother should take it. Fertility is new: a drug may affect sperm count or viability and contribute to teratogenicity. Lactation is new as well: children should not ingest certain medications in breast milk. She said, “F D A changed the labeling to include not just information on a drug's teratogenicity, but they now include information on fertility, especially male fertility, and also whether or not lactating women should take that medication.” The ones that do not belong are the therapeutic index, which is not part of the pregnancy and lactation labeling, and the margin of exposure, a pollutant value, not a drug label item.",
"L15-001": "The lungs are susceptible because of their oxidative burden, because the chemistry of an inhaled gas sets where in the lung it goes, and because particle size, shape and breathing pattern set where particles deposit. Inhaled oxygen and pollutant gases, plus the reactive oxygen species released by white blood cells, make the lung a very reactive place. She said, “these ideas of oxidative burden, the fact that we're exposed to different kinds of gasses, their chemistry is important, and particle size, shape, and our breathing patterns really affect our particle deposition.” The ones that do not belong are resident Kupffer cells, which are the liver's white blood cells, and most of the body's drug metabolism, which is the liver's job.",
"L15-002": "The oxidative burden comes from inhaled reactive gases and the white blood cells that respond to them. Oxygen and pollutant gases with oxygen in the molecule, such as sulfur dioxide and nitrogen oxides, become reactive as they are metabolized, and white blood cells drawn in by irritants or pathogens release reactive oxygen species as they work. She said, “breathing in oxygen and other what we call reactive oxygen species exposes our lungs to just nasty reactive compounds. So you have, therefore, what we call a big oxidative burden.” The tempting answer is hepatic first-pass metabolites carried into the lung; but first pass is a liver story, and the lung's burden comes from what it breathes and from its own immune response.",
"L15-003": "The property that decides how far down the airway a gas travels is its water solubility. The chemistry of the gas sets its site of deposition; in her words, very hydrophilic gases go quite far down into the lungs and hydrophobic gases do not, so looking at where a lung is damaged gives a hint about what the patient breathed. She said, “the chemistry of the gas will determine where in the lungs that gas goes. So, very hydrophilic gasses will actually go quite far down into the lungs and your hydrophobic gasses do not.” The tempting answer is the breathing rate alone; but breathing pattern changes particle deposition, and for gases the chemistry decides. Molecular weight and size are a particle story, and color and temperature have nothing to do with where a gas deposits.",
"L15-004": "Medium particles are the most concerning: heavy enough not to be breathed back out, small enough to turn the corners into the lower lung. She said, “the medium sized particles are the ones that are the most concerning. They are heavy enough to not be breathed out with every breath, and they are small enough that they can go around the curves in your bronchioles, and they get down into the lower parts of our lung.” The tempting answer is that large particles are carried deepest by the speed of inhalation; but large particles impact at the bifurcations of the upper airways, cannot turn the corners, and stick where they impact rather than going on to the alveoli.",
"L15-005": "The property that explains how asbestos reaches the outer lining of the lung is its long, thin, sharp shape. Long thin particles embed in the upper airways where the lung bifurcates, and some, like asbestos fibers, which are sharp, can move through the lung tissue to its outer lining and cause mesothelioma. Shape matters as well as size. She said, “those long thin particles will be also the ones that will be embedded into those upper airways as the lungs bifurcate, and some of those particles can move through tissues. I'm sure you guys have heard of mesothelioma with asbestos exposure.” The tempting answer is water solubility, but solubility is the gas story, and asbestos is a particle. Round particles do not embed the way long thin ones do.",
"L15-006": "Jogging in a park is better because heavier breathing while running deposits more exhaust particles in the lungs. Breathing patterns alter particle deposition: a runner breathes far more heavily than a walker, so the same polluted air delivers more to the lung. Her advice is to run where the air is cleaner. She said, “if you are walking, you're not going to breathe as heavily as you will if you're jogging. So I always like to suggest to people, jog in a place where there's not a lot of air pollution, so go jog in a park rather than beside a road.” The tempting answer is that exhaust gases are hydrophobic and stay in the upper airway; but the point is the breathing pattern, not the chemistry of exhaust.",
"L15-007": "The chronic responses to pulmonary exposures are fibrosis, emphysema and asthma. Fibrosis is an increase in collagen that reduces the plasticity of the lung. In emphysema the lungs become larger and too plastic, with hyperinflation, air trapping and reduced gas exchange from tissue loss. Asthma is counted as a chronic response. She said, “airway reactivity, pulmonary edema are acute, and then fibrosis, emphysema and asthma are considered chronic results of exposures.” The two that do not belong are the acute responses: airway reactivity, which is bronchoconstriction with wheezing, coughing, chest tightness and dyspnea, and pulmonary edema, the acute exudative phase that thickens the alveolar-capillary barrier, even though its abnormalities may persist.",
"L15-008": "Bleomycin and cyclophosphamide show that pulmonary toxicity can follow a systemic exposure, not only an inhaled one. Most pulmonary toxins are airborne, but some systemic compounds, including these two chemotherapy drugs given intravenously, damage the lungs, and so do the herbicides monocrotaline and paraquat. She said, “bleomycin and cyclophosphamide, these are chemotherapy medications. We give them by I V, but they're known to cause pulmonary toxicity. They're a systemic exposure. They cause pulmonary fibrosis.” The tempting answer treats intravenous drugs as airborne toxins because they reach the lung first; but they are systemic exposures, and the point is that the lung can be the target of something that never entered by inhalation.",
"L15-009": "Bleomycin's lung damage is non-organ specific because the damage is fibrosis, which is classed by its mechanism rather than by the organ. Fibrosis, excess collagen and extracellular matrix deposited until the tissue is destroyed, is a non-organ-specific endpoint wherever it occurs, like cancer, genotoxicity and teratogenesis; in the lung it is also a form of pulmonary toxicity. She flagged this as confusing on purpose. She said, “because of the mechanism of how that damage occurs, it's considered a non-organ specific. So I'm making it a little confusing for you right here. Fibrosis, both of these compounds cause fibrosis.” The tempting answer is that bleomycin is inhaled as an aerosol and so is an airborne pulmonary toxin; bleomycin is given intravenously.",
"L15-010": "The pulmonary response to fluorine or chlorine gas is acute and caustic, driven by the water in the lungs. Her answer to a student's question: a halogen gas is very irritating, definitely acute, and caustic in the wet lung, because it reacts with the water lining it. Chlorine gas releases from accidents and train derailments are historical examples. She said, “it would be definitely an acute response. It'll be very caustic to the lungs because of the water in our lungs. Fluorine gas, not a good thing to be exposed to. Like chlorine gas, any of those halogens in the periodic table.” The tempting answer is chronic fibrosis appearing years later; but fibrosis is a chronic response, and a halogen gas exposure is an acute one.",
"L15-011": "What makes the brain capillary a barrier is tight junctions in place of fenestrations, and tightly connected glial cells around it. Solutes cannot slip between the vessel cells, and the glial cells outside the vessel are tightly joined, unlike the loosely joined pericytes of a systemic capillary. She said, “instead of large fenestration sites, you've got very tight junctions where the cells of the vessel are tightly touching. You also have these brain capillaries surrounded by glial cells.” The tempting answer gives the brain capillary a thicker layer of loosely joined pericytes with larger fenestrations; but loosely joined pericytes and fenestrations are the systemic capillary, and the brain capillary is wrapped by tightly connected glial cells.",
"L15-012": "The correct statements are that penetration depends on a compound's lipophilicity, that the barrier is not continuous because some parts of the brain are looser than others, and that it is still developing at birth. Larger and non-lipophilic compounds cannot get through; and for months to years after birth it is immature, so drugs that stay out of an adult brain can enter a child's. She said, “it's protecting us from exposures to especially non-lipophilic compounds or larger compounds. There are parts of the brain where the blood-brain barrier is looser than others. That blood-brain barrier is still developing at birth.” The two that do not belong: it is not fully formed at birth, and it does not keep out lipophilic compounds preferentially.",
"L15-013": "The feature that explains harm to newborns is that the blood-brain barrier is still developing at birth. For months to years after birth the barrier is immature, so a drug that is kept out of the adult brain can cross into the child's. Pediatric dosing has to allow for an immature barrier. She said, “we would give some drugs to a child, a very young child. It would cross the blood-brain barrier and cause harm, whereas that same medication was not an issue for adults where their blood-brain barrier was more tight and contained.” The tempting answer says the barrier is tighter in children than in adults; it is the reverse, less complete in the newborn.",
"L15-014": "The nervous system is susceptible because of its very high energy requirements, axonal transport along very long cells, myelin, found in no other organ, which can be damaged, and its dependence on neurotransmission. Anything that reduces oxygen or energy available to neurons causes trouble, long axons must transport nutrients and waste, the Schwann-cell sheath is a target, and release, binding and reuptake of neurotransmitters can each be disturbed. She said, “high energy requirements, those long axons need to have the ability to transport nutrients and waste up and down them.” The two that do not belong are resident Kupffer cells, which belong to the liver, and a urine-concentrating mechanism, which is why the kidney is susceptible.",
"L15-015": "Tingling of the hands and feet on metronidazole is myelinopathy. Her own story: the drug damages the Schwann cells that make myelin, conduction slows, and the symptom is tingling of the hands and feet. She said, “it was actually the medication that I was being given. That medication will cause myelinopathy, and so, without talking to my doctor, I took myself off that drug. My hands and feet were tingling, it was strange.” The tempting answer is multiple sclerosis, her own first fear; but the cause was the medication, and stopping it resolved it. Death of whole neurons is not what tingling from a reversible drug effect reflects, the axon is not the primary target of this drug, and interruption of neurotransmission is the organophosphate pattern.",
"L15-016": "Neuronopathy is the entire neuron dying, axonopathy is the axon degenerating while the cell body survives, myelinopathy is damage to myelin or the myelinating cells, and neurotransmission toxicity is excitation blocked or excessive while the cell looks normal. She said, “what did that neurotoxin do to this nerve cell? Killed the whole thing. What kind of damage are we calling that? Axonopathy. How about cell D? Myelinopathy. The neuron in E, the neurotransmission is being disturbed.” The pair most easily confused is neuronopathy and axonopathy: when the whole cell dies it is neuronopathy, and when only the axon degenerates and the cell body survives it is axonopathy.",
"L15-017": "Organophosphate pesticides produce interruption of neurotransmission. That is her example for neuron E: a common class of pesticides that perturbs neurotransmission. She will not make the class memorize lists; the concept is that a neurotoxin typically causes one specific kind of damage, and her two testable examples are organophosphates and metronidazole. She said, “can you think of any sort of compound you've learned about that perturbs neurotransmission? It's a neurotoxin. How about organophosphates? A common class of pesticides that we use, they will cause neurotransmission damage.” The tempting answer is myelinopathy, but myelin is not their target; metronidazole was her myelinopathy example. It is very rare for one compound to cause more than one discrete pattern.",
"L15-018": "The pattern that leaves the neuron looking unchanged is interruption of neurotransmission. The cell is intact; what is missing is the neurotransmission, so it is recognized by loss of function instead. She said, “the neurotransmission really won't have any change to the look of the cell, but you'll definitely be able to figure that out because of the lack of neurotransmission going on.” The tempting answer is that every pattern changes the cell's appearance; the neurotransmission pattern does not. In neuronopathy the dead neuron and the gliosis around it are visible, in axonopathy the degenerated axon and chromatolysis are visible, and in myelinopathy the damaged sheath is visible.",
"L15-019": "The white blood cells that reside in the liver are the Kupffer cells. She spelled it out, K U P F E R: a kind of white blood cell that lives in the liver, waiting to be called up to clean up dead tissue or fight pathogens, and having its own inflammatory cells is one reason the liver is susceptible. She said, “does anybody know what the name of the white blood cells are that hang out in the liver? Kupffer cells, K U P F E R. They're just a kind of white blood cell that resides in the liver, waiting to be called up.” The tempting answer is hepatocytes, but hepatocytes are the metabolizing liver cells, not immune cells. Schwann cells make myelin in the peripheral nervous system, pericytes surround systemic capillaries, and astrocytes are glial cells of the nervous system.",
"L15-020": "Resident immune cells make the liver more susceptible because, cleaning up damaged cells, they release inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species. The cleanup adds inflammation and reactive oxygen to the injury already under way. She said, “the Kupffer cells are there to help, but kind of as a side effect of their job, they can create a little bit more of a mess by the inflammatory cytokines and reactive oxygen species they release doing their job of cleaning up pathogens or damaged cells.” The tempting answer is that they take up and concentrate xenobiotics from the portal blood; but uptake and concentration is a separate reason for susceptibility, and it belongs to the liver cells generally.",
"L15-021": "The organ most likely to show a severe, unexpected reaction in one patient is the liver, and what increasingly explains it is a rare genetic variant that changes the drug's metabolism. The liver is the organ most likely to have idiosyncratic reactions, because it does most of the body's metabolism, and pharmacogenomics is turning some of them into explained reactions. She said, “the liver is the organ in our body that tends to have that kind of reaction. They've got a genetic variant that's making them metabolize that drug differently than the rest of us, and perhaps that metabolism resulted in a nasty hepatotoxic metabolite.” The tempting answer is the liver because of its large size; but size has nothing to do with it, metabolism does.",
"L15-022": "Blood comes in by the hepatic artery and portal vein and leaves by the central vein. Blood trickles from the inflow vessels at the edge of the lobule down the cords of hepatocytes and drains into the central vein; the bile ducts also begin at the edges. She said, “you've got blood coming into each lobe through the hepatic artery and also the hepatic portal vein. The blood will trickle down across those cords of hepatocytes, and it drains out of the liver via the central vein.” The tempting answer brings blood in by the central vein and out by the bile duct; but the bile ducts begin at the edges and carry bile, not blood, and the central vein is the outflow.",
"L15-023": "The lesson is that most compounds cause one discrete kind of liver injury, and ethanol is unusual in causing many. Aflatoxin, causes liver cancer and little else, while ethanol can cause hepatocyte death, fatty liver, inflammation, altered regeneration and fibrosis. She said, “ethanol can cause fatty liver, also called steatosis. Ethanol can cause inflammation, affect liver regeneration, and cause fibrosis. Other compounds are not so good at hitting many different parts of the liver, so this aflatoxin really is only causing liver cancer.” The tempting answer is that ethanol is the most potent hepatotoxin; but potency is not what the table shows, breadth of response is.",
"L15-024": "Hepatocyte death is death of the cells that do most of the metabolism, as with acetaminophen and ethanol; steatosis is fatty liver, as with ethanol; canalicular cholestasis is bile flow blocked as the cells lining the canaliculi are damaged and inflamed; and liver cancer is the one response aflatoxin produces. She said, “hepatocyte death, that's the actual death of those hepatocytes, the ones that do a lot of the metabolism. The canalicular cholestasis means that the bile duct gets blocked, it gets narrowed because those cells lining the bile ducts get damaged and inflamed.” The pair most easily confused is hepatocyte death and steatosis: dead metabolizing cells is hepatocyte death, and fat in the liver is steatosis.",
"L15-025": "The pattern of liver injury is found by imaging and a liver biopsy, and the pattern hints at the exposure. Different hepatotoxins cause different, discrete kinds of damage, so the pattern of injury points toward what the patient was exposed to. When liver function is declining and the exposure is unknown, clinicians look for that pattern. She said, “often they'll do imaging and a liver biopsy to look for the pattern of injury, and that pattern of injury will give us a hint of what they might have been exposed to.” The tempting answer is liver function tests alone, with no biopsy once the enzymes are elevated; but liver function tests show only that the liver is affected, and the pattern of injury takes imaging and biopsy.",
"L15-026": "Zone three is closest to the central vein, where blood leaves. In the acinar model, zone one is next to the inflow vessels, zone two is intermediate, and zone three is next to the central venule drainage. The acinus follows the blood. She said, “zone one is closest to the vessels where the blood comes into the liver, and zone three is closest to the vessel where the blood leaves the liver.” The tempting answer puts zone three closest to the hepatic artery and portal vein, where blood enters; that is zone one. The intermediate region is zone two, the bile ducts begin at the edges next to zone one, and the region with the highest oxygen content is zone one, since zone three has the lowest.",
"L15-027": "The blood in zone three is relatively hypoxic because the hepatocytes upstream take oxygen out of it as it flows from the inflow vessels to the central vein. Blood arrives relatively oxygenated, the hepatocytes grab oxygen as it passes, and it leaves through the central vein relatively hypoxic. That oxygen gradient is one reason different hepatotoxins injure different zones. She said, “as the blood flows past those hepatocytes, and the hepatocytes grab the oxygen out of the blood, as that blood drains out of the liver via the central vein, the blood is hypoxic.” The tempting answer is that the central vein carries only venous blood from the gut that has already lost its oxygen; but the gradient forms inside the liver as hepatocytes use the oxygen.",
"L15-028": "Hepatocyte death from acetaminophen overdose shows in zone three, because cytochrome P four fifty two E one, which makes NAPQI, the toxic metabolite of acetaminophen, is most highly expressed there. Her little case: the overflow of acetaminophen is bioactivated by that enzyme, which is higher in zone three than in zone one, so the hepatocytes there die. She said, “what area of the liver has hepatocyte death occurring? Zone three. Why? Because that's where a lot of two E one is expressed.” The tempting answer is zone one, because it receives the drug first; but receiving the drug first is not what decides it, where the bioactivating enzyme lives does. Oxygen is high in zone one, but cytochrome P four fifty two E one is in zone three.",
"L15-029": "The correct statement is that metabolizing enzymes are not homogeneously expressed: many phase two enzymes sit in zone one, and cytochrome P four fifty two E one is higher in zone three. Enzyme expression is site-specific, which, together with the oxygen gradient, explains why hepatotoxins damage particular zones. She said, “a lot of our phase two enzymes are in zone one. And some of our P four fifties are more in zone three.” The tempting answer is that all cytochrome P four fifties are expressed evenly across the zones, so site-specific injury comes only from blood flow; that is the assumption she corrected, because expression is zonal.",
"L15-030": "The kidneys are susceptible because they receive a large delivery of blood including metabolites made by the liver, because concentrating urine also concentrates poisons in the tubule and the tubular cells, because of renal transport, accumulation and metabolism within the kidney cells, and because of sensitivity to vasoconstrictors acting on the afferent and efferent arterioles. She said, “it gets lots of blood flowing to it, especially blood that has gone through the liver. Like the liver, the cells in the kidney often will uptake compounds into themselves, so they're concentrating solutes.” The two that do not belong: the kidney has no barrier as tight as the blood-brain barrier, and Kupffer cells belong to the liver.",
"L15-031": "Vasoconstriction of the afferent and efferent arterioles reduces renal blood flow and lowers the glomerular filtration rate. Her unique-to-the-kidney point: constrict the arterioles and the glomerular filtration rate drops, and the concentration of what the kidney receives changes. She said, “it is sensitive to any vasoconstriction that will go on with the arterioles, both the afferent and the efferent arteriole. If you reduce that blood flow, you're going to reduce G F R.” The tempting answer is increased glomerular filtration from the higher pressure; but reduced flow lowers the filtration rate. Interstitial nephritis is an inflammatory, often allergic injury, not a flow effect.",
"L15-032": "The mechanisms of chemically induced acute renal failure on the table include vasoconstriction, crystalluria, tubular toxicity and interstitial nephritis. Vasoconstriction is the category she named for the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. Interstitial nephritis covers antibiotics, the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs and diuretics, and she adds the proton pump inhibitors. The compounds are not to be memorized; the kinds of renal damage are. She said, “do you need to memorize this table? No, what you need to know are these different kinds of renal damage just in general.” The two that do not belong are canalicular cholestasis, which is a liver response, and myelinopathy, which is a pattern of neurotoxicity.",
"L15-033": "The drug class she singled out is the non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs, and the advice is to limit their use and drink plenty of water. They are the class she worries about most: a common medication that can cause three kinds of renal injury, interstitial nephritis, vasoconstriction and glomerulopathy. She said, “one of the things I worry about the most are our non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs. They can cause interstitial nephritis. They should try to limit their use and drink lots of water while they're taking them.” The tempting answer is the proton pump inhibitors taken for at least a month; but the proton pump inhibitors cause interstitial nephritis, and the over-the-counter label limits their use to two weeks, not a month.",
"L15-034": "This is interstitial nephritis, an allergic response to the proton pump inhibitor, and the drug is stopped and never restarted. Her father's case: proton pump inhibitors cause interstitial nephritis, especially in patients with an aspirin allergy, and the allergy shows up in the kidney rather than as hives. She said, “proton pump inhibitors can cause interstitial nephritis too, especially in patients that have an aspirin allergy. He was having basically an allergic response to the proton pump inhibitor just like aspirin.” The tempting answer is tubular toxicity from drug concentrated in the tubule, with a lower dose and the drug continued; but the mechanism was immunologic, not concentration, and the drug had to be stopped for good.",
"L15-035": "The two reasons are that reflux needing more than two weeks should be checked, and that proton pump inhibitors can cause interstitial nephritis. Her father's months of unsupervised omeprazole are the illustration. She said, “it says limit your use to two weeks. If you need to use it for longer, you should consult a doctor. One reason is if you have reflux that's bad enough to need a proton pump inhibitor for more than two weeks, you probably should get it checked out. And another reason is the fact that these drugs can cause interstitial nephritis.” The tempting answer pairs crystalluria with raised blood pressure; but proton pump inhibitors were placed under interstitial nephritis, not crystalluria.",
"L15-036": "The difference she singled out as probably the most important is which metabolizing enzymes are expressed in which part of the kidney. Metabolism inside renal cells, varying along the nephron, sets where in-situ toxicity occurs. Tubular toxicity and crystalluria depend more on the chemistry of the drug itself. She said, “probably the most important reason is that you'll have all that metabolism going on in the renal cells, and it will depend on what enzymes are expressed in what part of the kidney to contribute to that site specificity.” Regional differences in blood flow is the tempting answer, since it is listed on the slide, but it is not the one she emphasized.",
"L15-037": "An idiosyncratic toxic response is a genetically determined, abnormally strong reaction in one person, not yet explained. The response is qualitatively like other people's but extreme, strong and unexpected, and genetically determined; as pharmacogenomics advances, some idiosyncratic reactions become explained reactions, traced to a rare genetic variant. She said, “idiosyncratic means it's a reaction that we don't know why it's happening always, and often idiosyncratic reactions are pretty strong. And sometimes unexpected.” The tempting answer is any reaction that occurs with low frequency, whatever its mechanism; that is the improper use of the term flagged on the slide.",
"L15-038": "The two broad categories of drug-triggered immune responses are hypersensitivity and autoimmune reactions. Hypersensitivity is the allergic response, types one through four, covered previously with Doctor Medina; an autoimmune reaction is the immune system turning on the patient's own body. She said, “why don't you change allergic to immunologic responses. So hypersensitivity is the first category, like a type one or a type two, type three, or type four type of allergic response. And then we see autoimmune reactions occurring.” The tempting answer is hapten formation and direct activation; but those are two ways a drug can be recognized as foreign, not the two categories of response.",
"L15-039": "A small-molecule drug triggers an immune response by acting as a hapten: bound to a body protein, the drug-protein complex is what the immune system responds to. Small molecules, under six hundred daltons, may act as haptens: the drug binds to certain body proteins, and the drug-protein complex is what the immune system recognizes and mounts a response against, while large drugs, therapeutic peptides and proteins, may activate the immune system directly. She said, “those small drugs, they may sometimes get bound up by certain proteins in the body, creating haptens, and that drug protein complex called a hapten can trigger the immune system to respond to it.” The tempting answer is direct activation of the immune system, the way a therapeutic protein does; but direct activation is how the large drugs, peptides and proteins, do it.",
"L15-040": "The therapies increasingly triggering autoimmune responses are the biologicals: protein and peptide therapies. Large drugs activate the immune system directly, and the resulting autoimmune responses are hard to predict and often bad. She said, “that is happening with more and more frequency as we continue to put on the market our biologicals. So our proteins, our peptide therapies are often the kinds of medications that will trigger the immune system to cause an autoimmune response.” The tempting answer is the small-molecule non-steroidal anti-inflammatory drugs taken for more than two weeks; but those are a kidney story here, not an autoimmune one.",
"L15-041": "The true statements are that a delayed response may be observed weeks to years after exposure, and that immediate effects can occur with each dose in a chronic exposure scenario. Her answer to the first was yes, with D E S and T O C P as the extreme examples; her answer to the last was yes, the worker who gets a headache every day from the same leak, with no tolerance. She said, “could a delayed response to a toxin be observed weeks to years after exposure? Yeah. Tissue injury from a toxic exposure is always reversible. No.” The three that are false: injury is not always reversible, since central nervous system injury, cancer and birth defects are irreversible; toxins do cause local effects; and systemic effects do require absorption.",
"L15-042": "The non-organ-specific toxic response on the list is teratogenicity. Her answer: teratogenicity is the one that is non-organ specific. Toxic endpoints are non-organ-specific, meaning fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis; organ-specific, meaning nervous system, lungs, liver and kidney; idiosyncratic; or immunologic. This was her second Poll Everywhere question, in her wording. She said, “neurotoxicity is the nerves. Pulmonary toxins in the lungs, nephrotoxins the kidneys. So teratogenicity is the one on this list that is non-organ specific.” The tempting answers each name an organ: neurotoxicity is the nerves, pulmonary toxicity is the lungs, and nephrotoxicity is the kidneys, so all three are organ-specific.",
"L15-043": "The liver is very susceptible because it expresses many enzymes that may metabolize drugs to toxic compounds, it receives a large volume of blood via the portal circulation, it is able to take up and concentrate xenobiotics, and it has its own immune cells to mount a quick inflammatory response. She said, “it expresses many enzymes that may metabolize drug to toxic compounds. That's right. It receives a large volume of blood via the portal circulation. Yes, that is true. It's a large organ, that really doesn't have anything to do with it. All of these were correct except for the fact that it's a large organ.” The one that does not belong is that it is a large organ: her answer was that being large really doesn't have anything to do with it.",
"L15-044": "One reason for regional susceptibility across the liver is the relatively high oxygen content in zone one. Her answer: zone one is the high-oxygen zone, and enzymes that need oxygen to do their metabolism may make the hepatotoxic metabolite there. She said, “is it zone one or zone three? Zone one. Yes, and if the enzymes that do metabolism there need oxygen to do their work, having a lot of oxygen right there is helpful, and unfortunately that metabolism may result in the metabolite that's hepatotoxic.” The tempting answer is homogeneous expression of P four fifties across the zones; but cytochrome P four fifty two E one is the example of an enzyme expressed in a particular zone, and most metabolizing enzymes are not homogeneous.",
"L15-045": "The blood concentration stays high because most of the drug is sequestered in lipid, muscle or bone and must move back into the blood before dialysis can reach it. Dialysis only clears what is in the blood; the reservoir in the tissues refills it, so dialysis has to run a long time. She said, “you have to wait for the drug to move out of the lipids, or out of the muscle or out of the bone back into the blood before hemodialysis is going to grab it again. So these people that have overdosed on drugs with very large volumes of distribution, you probably are going to have to do hemodialysis for a long time.” The tempting answer is that hemodialysis cannot remove any drug from the plasma; but dialysis does clear the plasma, and the problem is the inaccessible reservoir.",
"L15-046": "Blood concentrations are much higher than expected because liver metabolism is saturated, so the first-pass effect falls and more drug enters the circulation. Too much drug for the liver to handle goes right through unmetabolized. She said, “if you send to the liver a huge amount of that drug, and the liver's doing all the work it can to metabolize it, but you've got too much drug for the liver to handle, then that drug is going to go just right through the liver. It's not going to be metabolized, and it's gonna go into circulation.” The tempting answer is that the drug is absorbed directly into the systemic circulation, bypassing the portal vein and the liver entirely; but oral drug still reaches the liver first, and the liver just cannot keep up.",
"L15-047": "Iron overdose produces very high absorption because iron is caustic and damages the gut, removing the protection of saturable transport. With the gut wall blown apart, dissolved iron goes straight into the bloodstream. She said, “people that overdose on iron, iron is very caustic, it actually blows apart your gut. Therefore, you don't have the protection of a saturable active transport. You get iron going straight from the gut, it's dissolving in there, and then whoop, it's just getting right into the bloodstream.” The tempting answer is that the transporters are induced by the large dose; but induction is not the mechanism, tissue damage is.",
"L15-048": "The fraction of free drug rises, because plasma protein binding capacity is exceeded, and free drug is what causes toxicity. The plasma proteins are saturated, so more drug circulates unbound. Free drug is the fraction that acts, so the risk of toxicity rises. She said, “if a medication gets blood protein bound and you take a big dose, there's going to be more drug free in the blood, not bound up to the plasma proteins. Therefore, you've got a risk for having toxicity because a lot of that drug is free. You've oversaturated those plasma proteins.” The tempting answer is that the fraction is unchanged because binding is a fixed percentage; but the percentage holds only while binding capacity is not exceeded.",
"L15-049": "At the very high plasma concentrations of an overdose, elimination may change to zero order, extending the half-life. That is one of the clearance bullets on the kinetics slide, which she displayed and asked the class to read: high plasma concentrations may change elimination from first order to zero order. The other bullets on the same slide: slowed tablet dissolution or gastric emptying delays absorption and the peak toxic effect, and saturated liver metabolism reduces first pass. The tempting answer is first order with a shorter half-life; but the change is away from first order, and it lengthens the half-life. Second order is not a kinetic order on the slide, and elimination continues, at a fixed amount per unit time, rather than stopping.",
"L15-050": "Drugs A and B are alike at the therapeutic endpoint, but in overdose their therapeutic indices differ and B can be pushed higher without an unwanted effect. Her answer was B: increasing the dose of A leads to coma, while B plateaus before coma. Both sedate at the same dose, so they look alike therapeutically, but A rises in a straight line to coma and B levels off at anesthesia. She said, “which one can you increase the dose and not have an unwanted adverse effect? B. So, drug A, if you increase the dose more, you're getting to an unwanted effect.” The tempting answer is that they behave identically at every dose because they share a mechanism and a therapeutic dose; but they look the same only at the therapeutic endpoint, and only A reaches coma on the figure.",
"L15-051": "A tricyclic antidepressant overdose is so dangerous pharmacodynamically because it reduces sweating while increasing muscle activity and the risk of seizures. Two effects that are not part of therapy combine: heat builds from muscle activity and seizures, and the body cannot shed it by sweating. She said, “the tricyclics, they reduce sweating. In an overdose situation, that's one of their pharmacodynamic effects, at the same time that they are increasing muscular activity and potential for seizures. So it's a double whammy in an overdose situation.” The tempting answer is reduced blood flow to the kidney and liver, so the drug is eliminated more slowly; but that is the beta-blocker and calcium channel blocker example.",
"L15-052": "In a beta-blocker or calcium channel blocker overdose, the effect that keeps the dose high is reduced blood flow to the kidney and liver, so the drug is eliminated more slowly. The organs of elimination are underperfused, clearance slows, and the concentration stays up. She said, “reducing blood flow to the kidneys and the liver, you're reducing the body's ability to eliminate the drug. You're also reducing blood flow to your organs of elimination, and therefore you can't eliminate the drugs very well, and the dose stays high.” The tempting answer is saturation of plasma protein binding; but that is a kinetic change, not this pharmacodynamic one. Increased sweating is the tricyclic example in the other direction.",
"L15-053": "The three basic approaches are toxicokinetic-based treatment, inactivation of the poison, and pharmacologically based treatment. Treat the kinetics, inactivate the poison, or counter its pharmacodynamics. She said, “we will either think about treating them based on kinetics, or we'll think about treating them based on trying to inactivate the poison. Or we will think about trying to treat them based on, basically the pharmacology, the pharmacodynamics of what's going on.” The tempting answer is prevent absorption, inhibit bioactivation, enhance metabolism, enhance elimination; but those are the four toxicokinetic methods inside one approach, not the three approaches. Emesis, lavage and charcoal are three methods within preventing absorption.",
"L15-054": "The toxicokinetic-based methods are prevention of absorption, inhibition of toxication, which is bioactivation, enhancement of metabolism, which is detoxification, and enhancement of elimination. Inhibiting toxication stops the poison being converted to a nastier compound, and enhancing metabolism speeds the conversion to something less harmful. She said, “sometimes we will try to prevent absorption of the poison. Sometimes we're going to try to inhibit toxication or stop the bioactivation of it to a nasty compound. Sometimes we can enhance metabolism or enhance detoxification.” The two that do not belong are giving a receptor antagonist, which is a pharmacologically based treatment, and binding the poison with a chelator, which is inactivation of the poison.",
"L15-055": "The methods used to prevent absorption of an ingested poison are emesis, gastric lavage, whole bowel irrigation, single-dose activated charcoal and multiple-dose activated charcoal. Emesis is no longer used, and multiple-dose charcoal is for compounds that recirculate through the bile. She said, “try to cause emesis, which is vomiting. We could do gastric lavage. How about whole bowel irrigation? We also could try to prevent absorption using activated charcoal, single dose or multiple dose activated charcoal.” The two that do not belong: hemodialysis enhances elimination of a poison already absorbed, and sodium bicarbonate to trap a weak acid in the urine is ion trapping, which enhances renal elimination.",
"L15-056": "Syrup of ipecac is no longer sold in the United States or used in hospitals, and the biggest risk of induced emesis is aspiration. Vomiting can send stomach contents into the lungs. She said, “they no longer even sell it in the U S. They don't even use it anymore in hospitals, because it's too risky. What's the risk of emesis? Aspiration is the biggest risk.” The tempting answer keeps ipecac as first-line in emergency rooms for children with dehydration as the risk; but it is no longer used, and aspiration, not dehydration, is the risk she named. It is not sold over the counter, esophageal rupture was a lavage concern, lavage is a hospital procedure rather than a home remedy, and no niche use for extended-release tablets was described.",
"L15-057": "In gastric lavage, a tube is passed to the stomach, fluid is pumped in and sucked out, and large tablets may not come back up the tube. The risks: the tube can end up in the lungs and fluid can be pumped into them, and it is dangerous in anyone with esophageal rupture or stomach problems. She said, “pump in your stomach, put a tube down, hope you don't get that tube in the person's lungs. So gastric lavage, you don't wanna do it for anybody that has any sort of esophageal rupture, issues with their stomach, all that dangerous.” The tempting answer equates it with whole bowel irrigation flushing the entire gut with polyethylene glycol; but irrigation flushes the whole gut from above, as for a colonoscopy, while lavage washes the stomach only.",
"L15-058": "Whole bowel irrigation is the same procedure as colonoscopy preparation: drinking a polyethylene glycol solution until it passes through. The solution is a polyethylene glycol electrolyte solution such as GoLYTELY, and the whole gut is flushed. She said, “has anybody undergone a colonoscopy? That's whole bowel irrigation. You drink something like GoLYTELY, polyethylene glycol, or mag citrate or something. You've got to make sure people don't have any issues, no ileus, no blockages in the gut.” The tempting answer is gastric lavage with a nasogastric tube; but lavage washes the stomach only, while irrigation flushes the whole gut. Charcoal adsorbs the poison rather than flushing it, dialysis works on the blood rather than the gut, and ipecac empties the stomach upward.",
"L15-059": "Activated charcoal adsorbs only certain compounds, and what it binds leaves in the feces. Its chemistry decides what sticks, and if the poison does not stick, charcoal is pointless. It is purified charcoal with very fine particles, given as a slurry in water; the main risk is that the patient vomits and aspirates. She said, “this is purified charcoal. It's got very fine particles. Not every compound a person ingests is going to be grabbed and absorbed by charcoal. Where does it go? Fecal elimination.” The tempting answer is that it binds every ingested compound; not every compound is grabbed by charcoal. The bound drug is not absorbed into the blood; charcoal is not absorbed, and the complex goes out in the feces.",
"L15-060": "Single-dose activated charcoal probably needs to be given within one to two hours of the ingestion. After that the drug has passed the stomach and is being absorbed into the bloodstream. A single dose has to reach the stomach while the poison is still there, so it is given very soon after ingestion. She said, “single dose activated charcoal, we say it probably needs to be done within one to two hours of somebody ingesting whatever they overdosed on.” The tempting answer is any time, on the idea that charcoal pulls drug back out of the blood; but a single dose works only on what is still in the stomach. Ten to fifteen minutes is shorter than the window she gave, and six to eight hours or twenty four hours is far too late, because the drug has moved on.",
"L15-061": "Multiple-dose activated charcoal is reserved for a compound that sticks to charcoal and undergoes enterohepatic recirculation. A later dose catches drug that the liver secretes back into the small intestine through the bile. She said, “if you give a second dose of charcoal, you might be able to catch some of that compound as it's being re-injected back into the small intestine from the liver, through the bile. The use of multiple dose activated charcoal is limited to things that get stuck on charcoal and undergo that recirculation.” The tempting answer is a compound swallowed more than twenty four hours ago; but timing does not create the indication, recirculation does.",
"L15-062": "The poison control centers have said that these methods are risky and should be used only when really necessary, at the judgment of the emergency team. The methods remain available to experienced toxicologists for massive, dangerous ingestions, but none is routine: poison control centers in America and Europe have all issued statements calling them dangerous, so the choice rests with the emergency room. She said, “they all basically say they're dangerous, they're risky, only use them if you really think you have to. So then it's up to the judgment of the people in the E R.” The tempting answer is that they are banned in hospitals with reporting required; but they are discouraged, not banned, and the emergency room decides.",
"L15-063": "The evidence comes from published case reports and case series, evaluated and graded by expert authors. Clinicians write up real overdoses, and experts pull every report, evaluate each one and grade the evidence and the recommendations, as in any other guideline. She said, “the data for these evidence-based guidance documents comes from people overdosing. Clinicians, pharmacists, doctors write up the case, get it published. These people that wrote these guidance documents are experts in the field. They pulled all those documents, evaluated every single one of those case reports or case series.” The tempting answer is randomized clinical trials in consenting volunteers; but no one can ethically be consented to overdose.",
"L15-064": "Evidence-based consensus guidelines for out-of-hospital management have been written for beta-blockers, calcium channel blockers, acetaminophen, iron and tricyclic antidepressants. Beta-blockers and calcium channel blockers are the two documents the class will discuss next; acetaminophen, iron and the tricyclics are on the slide's list. She said, “only a few drugs are out there that have these documents written, but these are pretty nasty drugs. And we're going to just look at two of these documents, beta blockers and calcium channel blockers.” The two that do not belong are metronidazole, which is not on the list and was her myelinopathy example, and omeprazole, which is not on the list and was her interstitial nephritis example.",
"L15-065": "The two differences are beta-blocker versus calcium channel blocker management, and immediate-release versus extended-release tablets. The two guidance documents say what to do when a patient has swallowed a handful of beta-blocker or a handful of calcium channel blocker; the abstracts summarize what to do. She said, “there are slight differences in what we do for a beta blocker overdose compared to a calcium channel overdose, even though they're all cardiac meds. It also has differences between immediate release and extended release tablets, what we do.” The tempting answer is single- versus multiple-dose charcoal and emesis versus lavage; but those are absorption-prevention distinctions, not the guideline contrasts she named."
});
